</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Alexander Rakhlin
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>MIT
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">rakhlin@mit.edu<span id="id3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">
</span></span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Understanding minimal assumptions that enable learning and generalization is perhaps the central question of learning theory.
Several celebrated results in statistical learning theory, such as the VC theorem and Littlestone’s characterization of online learnability, establish conditions on the hypothesis class that allow for learning under independent data and adversarial data, respectively.
Building upon recent work bridging these extremes, we study sequential decision making under <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">distributional adversaries</em> that can adaptively choose data-generating distributions from a fixed family <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and ask when such problems are learnable with sample complexity that behaves like the favorable independent case.
We provide a near complete characterization of families <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> that admit learnability in terms of a notion known as <em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generalized smoothness</em> i.e.
a distribution family admits VC-dimension-dependent regret bounds for every finite-VC hypothesis class if and only if it is generalized smooth.
Further, we give universal algorithms that achieve low regret under any generalized smooth adversary without explicit knowledge of <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, when <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is known, we provide refined bounds in terms of a combinatorial parameter, the fragmentation number, that captures how many disjoint regions can carry nontrivial mass under <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
These results provide a nearly complete understanding of learnability under distributional adversaries.
In addition, building upon the surprising connection between online learning and differential privacy, we show that the generalized smoothness also characterizes private learnability under distributional constraints.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The grand challenge of statistics and learning theory is to understand when data can be used to make accurate predictions.
Various formalizations of this question have been studied over the decades, leading to a rich theory of learnability under different data-generating assumptions on the covariates and the labels, performance metrics, feedback models, and computational constraints.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One prominent framework is <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">PAC learning</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> which supposes that data are drawn independently and identically from a distribution.
In this setting, the celebrated VC theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> shows that a hypothesis class <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is learnable with sample complexity depending on its VC dimension <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>.
On the other extreme of the data-generating spectrum lies <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">online learning</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, which models data as being completely arbitrarily chosen (modeled as being chosen by an adversary).
Here, Littlestone’s seminal work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> established that a hypothesis class <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is learnable with regret depending on its Littlestone dimension <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>.
Though these two frameworks provide a solid understanding of learnability under independent data (which we view as “benign” or well-behaved in our present context but consider a strong and perhaps unreasonable modelling assumption) and adversarial data (which is a minimal assumption that treats the data as malicious and ill-behaved), real-world applications typically involve data-generating processes that lie between these two extremes.
Further, and perhaps unsurprisingly, the characterization of learnability under the fully adversarial model of Littlestone precludes learnability even for simple hypothesis classes such as linear thresholds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This motivates the study of structured data-generating processes that interpolate between the two extremes of i.i.d. and fully adversarial data.
A natural formulation is to consider a process where the choice of data at each round is constrained to lie within a certain family of distributions.
This framework is referred to as <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">distributionally constrained online learning</em>.
Perhaps the most well-studied special case is that of <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">smoothed adversaries</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, where the adversary is constrained to choose distributions that have bounded density ratio with respect to a fixed reference measure.
This line of work, which can be also be seen as a model for distribution shift (See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> and references therein), has established that, under smoothness, sequential decision-making has the same qualitative learnability guarantees as in the i.i.d. setting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>.
Though this gives a satisfactory answer under smoothness, perhaps the condition is too strong, leading to the following fundamental question:</p>
<blockquote id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.p3.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">What are minimal conditions on a distribution family <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> that ensure any form of learnability for low-complexity function classes (e.g., finite VC dimension), against adaptive <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>-distributionally constrained adversaries?</em></p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Surprisingly, this question is intimately connected to a seemingly unrelated problem of learning under <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">privacy</em> constraints.
Differential privacy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> is a rigorous framework for ensuring that learning algorithms do not leak sensitive information about individual data points.
Unfortunately, differential privacy, being a stringent condition often imposes significant limitations on what can be learned, perhaps most notably exemplified by the fact that for binary classification, only classes with finite Littlestone dimension are privately learnable <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.
As with online learning, this negative result has motivated the study of structured data-generating processes that allow for private learnability of richer classes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
We ask whether there is a characterization of distribution families that admit private learnability with finite sample complexity for VC hypothesis classes.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Our Contribution.</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We address this question by providing a complete characterization of distribution families <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> that admit learning for VC classes. To do this, we introduce a structural condition on <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> that generalizes the classical smoothness condition from smoothed online learning.
Recall that in smoothed online learning, the adversary is constrained to choose distributions that have bounded density ratio with respect to a fixed reference measure <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>; that is, there exists <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that for every <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and measurable set <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(A)\leq\mu_{0}(A)/\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(A)\leq\mu_{0}(A)/\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.
<em id="S1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Generalized Smoothness</em> relaxes this linear relationship to allow for more general tolerance functions.
That is, a distribution family <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\rho,\mu_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\rho,\mu_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>-<em id="S1.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generalized</em> smooth if for every <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mu(A)\leq\rho(\mu_{0}(A))\quad\text{for all measurable sets }A." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>for all measurable sets </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mu(A)\leq\rho(\mu_{0}(A))\quad\text{for all measurable sets }A.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This condition implies that the adversary cannot place significant mass on sets that are small under the base measure—but the relationship need not be linear as in classical smoothness.
Our main result (<a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) shows that this condition is necessary for any form of learnability against every finite-VC class <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In fact, we show a stronger result: generalized smoothness is necessary even to achieve asymptotic consistency against thresholds, the simplest non-trivial function classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To complete the characterization, we build upon recent algorithmic techniques for smoothed online learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> to show that generalized smoothness is sufficient to achieve VC-dimension-dependent regret bounds for every finite-VC class <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, even when <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is not explicitly known to the learner (<a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ Agnostic setting. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorems</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 6. ‣ Realizable setting. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).
These results show that the complex interplay of combinatorial structure and regret bound for distributionally constrained adversaries can be fully captured by a single structural condition on the distribution family <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. Additionally, and perhaps surprisingly, this also shows that merely asking for asymptotic consistency on simple VC classes further implies non-asymptotic regret bounds. Note that asymptotic and non-asymptotic learnability are, in general, qualitatively different notions with distinct characterizations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our main technical contribution is establishing a connection between regret bound against threshold classes to a uniform notion of compactness of the distribution family <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, which we capture via the existence of a continuous dominating submeasure (<a href="#Thmdefinition6" title="Definition 6 (Continuous Submeasure). ‣ 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) for <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Further, we show that the existence of such a continuous dominating submeasure is equivalent to generalized smoothness.
The relation between continuity of submeasures and uniform domination by a measure (corresponding to generalized smoothness) is well-studied in measure theory, with celebrated results such as the <em id="S1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Kalton-Roberts theorem</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Interestingly, this result is perhaps the first significant example of the use of expander graphs outside graph theory</span></span></span> and (Talagrand’s resolution of) <em id="S1.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Maharam’s problem</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>.
In our setting, with the extra structure of our submeasure (arising as a supremum over a family of probability measures), we are able to establish a clean equivalence between continuity and uniform domination directly without relying on opaque application of this heavy machinery but developing a deeper connection to these classical results is an interesting direction for future work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We complement the characterization with algorithms (<a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 6. ‣ Realizable setting. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorems</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ Agnostic setting. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) that achieve VC-dimension-dependent regret bounds when <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is generalized smooth, even when <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is unknown to the learner.
These can be viewed as the generalized smoothness analogues of the algorithms for smoothed online learning with unknown base measure from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.
Finally, when <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is known to the learner, we provide refined regret bounds (<a href="#Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 10. ‣ 5 Distribution-class-dependent regret bounds ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorems</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 8. ‣ 5 Distribution-class-dependent regret bounds ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) that depend on a combinatorial parameter of <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> that we term the <em id="S1.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fragmentation number</em> (<a href="#Thmdefinition8" title="Definition 8 (Fragmentation number). ‣ 5 Distribution-class-dependent regret bounds ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).
While having finite fragmentation numbers is equivalent to generalized smoothness, the precise dependence of regret bounds on fragmentation numbers provides a more refined understanding of the difficulty of learning under a given distribution family <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to the different rates in the <em id="S1.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">known-base-measure</em> smoothed online learning setting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> and the <em id="S1.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unknown-base-measure</em> setting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to the characterization of online learnability in the distribution constrained setting, generalized smoothness serves as a characterization of private learnability under the distributionally constrained settings.
This can be seen as the distributionally constrained analogue of the characterization of private learnability via the Littlestone dimension <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.
We show that a distribution family <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> admits private learnability with sample complexity depending only on the VC dimension of the hypothesis class if and only if <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is generalized smooth (<a href="#Thmtheorem11" title="Theorem 11. ‣ 6 Differential Privacy ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorems</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem12" title="Theorem 12 (Private Learnability under Generalized Smoothness). ‣ 6 Differential Privacy ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be a separable measurable instance space and denote by <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> the collection of measurable sets of <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}=\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}=\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the label space.
Let <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\subseteq\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\subseteq\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> be a family of measurable binary classifiers, associated with indicators over sets, and use the indicator loss <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbbm{1}\{f(x)\neq y\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{1}\{f(x)\neq y\}.</annotation></semantics></math>
While we focus on the case of binary classification and the indicator loss for concreteness, extending ideas proposed here to multiclass classification and regression is an interesting direction for future work.
The central combinatorial quantity in the binary classification setting is the VC dimension <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(VC dimension)<span id="Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The VC dimension of a function class <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{VC}(\mathcal{F})\;=\;\sup\left\{d\in\mathbb{N}:\exists x_{1},\dots,x_{d}\in\mathcal{X}\text{ s.t. }|\{(f(x_{1}),\dots,f(x_{d})):f\in\mathcal{F}\}|=2^{d}\right\}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>VC</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> s.t. </mtext><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{VC}(\mathcal{F})\;=\;\sup\left\{d\in\mathbb{N}:\exists x_{1},\dots,x_{d}\in\mathcal{X}\text{ s.t. }|\{(f(x_{1}),\dots,f(x_{d})):f\in\mathcal{F}\}|=2^{d}\right\}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A set <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{1},\dots,x_{d}\}\subseteq\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{1},\dots,x_{d}\}\subseteq\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the above condition is said to be <em id="Thmdefinition1.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">shattered</em> by <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We next define the online learning protocol under distributional constraints.
Let <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> be a family of probability distributions over <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider the following <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-round online protocol. For each round <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,\dots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,\dots,T</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The adversary selects a distribution <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, either <em id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">obliviously</em> (the sequence <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu_{t})_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu_{t})_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed in advance) or <em id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">adaptively</em> based on the history.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The learner chooses <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}\in\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}\in\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> (possibly randomized).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A sample <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}\sim\mu_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}\sim\mu_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is drawn; then a label <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> is revealed. The learner incurs loss <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbbm{1}\{f_{t}(x_{t})\neq y_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{1}\{f_{t}(x_{t})\neq y_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math> (and, in the full-information setting, observes <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{t},y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{t},y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>). Note that <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is not observed.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The objective of the learner is to minimize regret against the comparator class <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, the difference between the cumulative loss of the learner and that of the best fixed function in <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> in hindsight. Formally, define the regret after <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> rounds for an algorithmn <span id="S2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">alg</span> as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{Reg}_{T}(\text{alg};\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U})\,=\,\mathbb{E}\Bigg[\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}\{f_{t}(x_{t})\neq y_{t}\}\,-\,\inf_{f\in\mathcal{F}}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}\{f(x_{t})\neq y_{t}\}\Bigg]\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Reg</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>alg</mtext><mo>;</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.448em">=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.114em">−</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></munder><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Reg}_{T}(\text{alg};\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U})\,=\,\mathbb{E}\Bigg[\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}\{f_{t}(x_{t})\neq y_{t}\}\,-\,\inf_{f\in\mathcal{F}}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}\{f(x_{t})\neq y_{t}\}\Bigg]\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The setup descibed above generalizes both the classical adversarial online learning setting (when <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> contains all distributions over <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>) and the (sequential analogue of the) standard i.i.d. statistical learning setting (when <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is a single fixed distribution).
We denote the best achievable regret against the worst-case <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>-constrained adversary by <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Reg}_{T}(\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Reg</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Reg}_{T}(\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that when <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is a singleton, from the VC theory of statistical learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, we have <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Reg}_{T}(\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U})\lesssim\sqrt{\mathrm{VC}(\mathcal{F})T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Reg</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>VC</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Reg}_{T}(\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U})\lesssim\sqrt{\mathrm{VC}(\mathcal{F})T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Futher, when <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> contains all distributions over <math id="S2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, from the classical theory of adversarial online learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, we have that <math id="S2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Reg}_{T}(\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U})\lesssim\sqrt{\mathrm{LD}(\mathcal{F})T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Reg</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>LD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Reg}_{T}(\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U})\lesssim\sqrt{\mathrm{LD}(\mathcal{F})T}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{LD}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>LD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{LD}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> is the Littlestone dimension of <math id="S2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite><span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          Since we do not directly work with the Littlestone dimension, we defer the definition to the appendix (<a href="#Thmdefinition10" title="Definition 10 (Littlestone Dimension). ‣ B.1 Additional definitions ‣ Appendix B Further preliminaries ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>)</span></span></span>.
The key issue we aim to address is the fact that <math id="S2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{LD}\gg\mathrm{VC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>LD</mi><mo>≫</mo><mi>VC</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{LD}\gg\mathrm{VC}</annotation></semantics></math> for most natural function classes, with <math id="S2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{LD}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LD</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{LD}</annotation></semantics></math> typically being infinite.
With this is mind, the aim of this work is to characterize conditions on <math id="S2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> that allow achieving regret bounds that depend on the VC dimension of <math id="S2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In order to capture this, we introduce two notions of learnability for distribution families.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Weak VC Learnability)<span id="Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A distribution class <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is weakly VC learnable if for any function class <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> with finite VC dimension, there exists an online algorithm <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="alg" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">alg</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>-constrained adversary generating a data sequence <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{t},y_{t})_{t\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{t},y_{t})_{t\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> with the label <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> consistent with some function in <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lim_{T\to\infty}\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}[\hat{y}_{t}\neq y_{t}]\right]=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{T\to\infty}\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}[\hat{y}_{t}\neq y_{t}]\right]=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the definition above merely asks for <em id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">asymptotic</em> consistency on realizable data: convergence rates are allowed to potentially depend on the adversary’s behavior.
Arguably, this is the weakest meaningful learning guarantee. To emphasize the difference between asymptotic consistency and finite-regret bounds, we note that any countable function class <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>—in particular, even with infinite VC dimension—admits asymptotically consistent learners even when the distribution class <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is fully unrestricted, i.e., even for adversarial data (this is for instance a direct consequence of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, Lemma 34]</cite>).
We can strengthen the notion of learnability by requiring <em id="S2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">non-asymptotic</em> regret bounds that depend on the VC dimension of the comparator class.
We will refer to this as <em id="S2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">strong VC learnability</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Strong VC Learnability)<span id="Thmdefinition3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A distribution class <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is strongly VC learnable if for any function class <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> with finite VC dimension, for any <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> there exists <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon,d)</annotation></semantics></math> and an online algorithm <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="alg" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">alg</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>-constrained adversary, and <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\geq m_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\geq m_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon,d)</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Reg}_{T}(\text{alg};\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U})\leq\epsilon T." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Reg</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">alg</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Reg}_{T}(\text{alg};\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U})\leq\epsilon T.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The key structural condition that captures learnability is <em id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generalized smoothness</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Generalized smoothed distribution class)<span id="Thmdefinition4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be a measurable space.
Fix a measure <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and a non-decreasing function <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:[0,1]\to\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:[0,1]\to\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\epsilon\to 0}\rho(\epsilon)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\epsilon\to 0}\rho(\epsilon)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
A family of distributions <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu_{0},\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu_{0},\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>-generalized smoothed if for any distribution <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and measurable set <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subseteq\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subseteq\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(A)\leq\rho(\mu_{0}(A))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(A)\leq\rho(\mu_{0}(A)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">One can see that generalized smoothness implies that the distributions in <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> are absolutely continuous with respect to the base measure <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> acting as a uniform control on this continuity.
Note that this definition generalizes classical smoothness, which corresponds to the special case <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\epsilon)=\epsilon/\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\epsilon)=\epsilon/\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> for some constant <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Further, this notion also generalizes smoothness notions based on <math id="S2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergences studied in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>.
Last, the notion of generalized smoothness is closely related to the notion of coverage studied by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>.
All these notions control the mass in the tails of density ratios between distributions in <math id="S2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and a base measure <math id="S2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and can be seen as different parameterizations of the same underlying concept.
However, the notion of generalized smoothness is the most convenient for our purposes here and most directly addresses the underlying question of learnability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Learnability and Generalized Smoothness</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we characterize weak and strong VC learnability as defined in <a href="#Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2 (Weak VC Learnability). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definitions</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#Thmdefinition3" title="Definition 3 (Strong VC Learnability). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Specifically, we show that both are equivalent to generalized smoothness: in particular, weak VC learnability is—despite its asymptotic nature by definition—sufficient to recover non-asymptotic regret bounds. In fact, it turns out that weak learnability with respect to simple VC=1 classes is already sufficient for non-asymptotic regret bounds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To formalize this result, we begin by introducing the family of <em id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generalized thresholds</em>, which are the prototypical example of VC one classes and conceptually, the simplest non-trivial function classes for learning.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Generalized thresholds)<span id="Thmdefinition5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A function class <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="Thmdefinition5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">generalized threshold</em> function class if there exists a total preorder<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">3</span></span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              <span id="footnote3.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">Preorders satisfy the same properties as orders except for antisymmetry: </span><math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\preceq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⪯</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\preceq</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote3.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> is a preorder if it is reflexive and transitive.</span></span></span></span> <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\preceq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⪯</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\preceq</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{F}\subseteq\{\mathbbm{1}[\cdot\preceq x_{0}]:x_{0}\in\mathcal{X}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">⪯</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\subseteq\{\mathbbm{1}[\cdot\preceq x_{0}]:x_{0}\in\mathcal{X}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These function classes naturally generalize the standard thresholds— <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{\mathbbm{1}[\cdot\leq x_{0}]:x_{0}\in[0,1]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">≤</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbbm{1}[\cdot\leq x_{0}]:x_{0}\in[0,1]\}</annotation></semantics></math>—on the interval <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> to the case when one may use potentially different orderings than <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math> on the domain <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that the VC dimension of any generalized threshold class is at most <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Due to their simplicity, we use generalized thresholds as “test classes” for learnability.
We specialize the notion of weak learnability (<a href="#Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2 (Weak VC Learnability). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) and strong learnability (<a href="#Thmdefinition3" title="Definition 3 (Strong VC Learnability). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) to generalized thresholds and refer to these as <em id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">weak threshold learnability</em> and <em id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">strong threshold learnability</em>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Surprisingly, we show in this section that weak threshold learnability is equivalent to generalized smoothness and hence sufficient to recover non-asymptotic regret-bounds for all function classes with finite VC dimension. In turn, this will imply the equivalence of weak and strong VC learnability to generalized smoothness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As the first step of this proof, we study links between weak threshold learnability of a distribution class <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and properties of its envelope functional <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> defined via</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A):=\sup_{\mu\in\mathcal{U}}\mu(A),\quad A\in\mathcal{B}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A):=\sup_{\mu\in\mathcal{U}}\mu(A),\quad A\in\mathcal{B}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">A key property will be whether <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is additionally a continuous submeasure, as defined below.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Continuous Submeasure)<span id="Thmdefinition6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A function <math id="Thmdefinition6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu}:\mathcal{B}\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}:\mathcal{B}\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="Thmdefinition6.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">continuous submeasure</em> if:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Monotonicity:</span><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> For any </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,B\in\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,B\in\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subseteq B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subseteq B</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu}(A)\leq\hat{\mu}(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}(A)\leq\hat{\mu}(B)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Sub-additivity:</span><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> For any </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,B\in\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,B\in\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu}(A\cup B)\leq\hat{\mu}(A)+\hat{\mu}(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}(A\cup B)\leq\hat{\mu}(A)+\hat{\mu}(B)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Continuity from above:</span><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> For any decreasing sequence of measurable sets </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A_{i})_{i\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A_{i})_{i\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcap_{i\geq 1}A_{i}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⋂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcap_{i\geq 1}A_{i}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, we have </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{n\to\infty}\hat{\mu}(A_{n})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{n\to\infty}\hat{\mu}(A_{n})=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Of course, the functional <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> always satisfies monotonicity and sub-additivity for any distribution class <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. More importantly, we show that <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> must also satisfy the continuity from above property if the distribution class <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is weakly threshold learnable.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> be a weakly threshold learnable distribution class on <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a continuous submeasure.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">As a brief overview of the proof, if <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> fails to be continuous, then there exist infinitely-many (countably) disjoint sets each carrying at least <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> mass under some distribution in <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> for some parameter <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. We index these sets by rationals and construct a threshold class where each function <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator of the union of sets indexed by rationals at most <math id="S3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. By construction, the adversary can place <math id="S3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> mass under each of these sets, which allows the adversary to simulate an unconstrained adversary on the threshold function class. This yields an expected lower bound <math id="S3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(\epsilon T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(\epsilon T)</annotation></semantics></math> on the number of mistakes following classical lower bound arguments for adversarial online learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, up to minor modifications to ensure realizability. Essentially, the adversary hides the true threshold <math id="S3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> through a binary search.
We defer the full proof to <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Proofs from ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The main step of the proof is to show that if <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a continuous submeasure, then the distribution class <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to generalized smoothed classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> be a family of distributions on <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a continuous submeasure. Then, <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu_{0},\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu_{0},\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>-generalized smooth for some distribution <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and a non-decreasing function <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:[0,1]\to\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:[0,1]\to\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\epsilon\to 0}\rho(\epsilon)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\epsilon\to 0}\rho(\epsilon)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The proof is constructive. At the high-level, if <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous then for any scale <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> then we can construct a finite cover <math id="S3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mu_{1},\ldots,\mu_{N}\}\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mu_{1},\ldots,\mu_{N}\}\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> of the distribution class <math id="S3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> for any measurable set <math id="S3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> on which some distribution <math id="S3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> places <math id="S3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> mass. Taking an appropriate mixture of these finite covers for decreasing scales <math id="S3.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> then yields a distribution <math id="S3.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> with the desired generalized-smoothness property on <math id="S3.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof  </span>Fix <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the assumptions and any <math id="S3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. We iteratively construct a sequence of distributions <math id="S3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu_{i}^{\epsilon})_{i\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu_{i}^{\epsilon})_{i\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> together with measurable sets <math id="S3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A_{i}^{\epsilon})_{i\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A_{i}^{\epsilon})_{i\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows. Suppose we have constructed <math id="S3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{j}^{\epsilon},A_{j}^{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{j}^{\epsilon},A_{j}^{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&lt;i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&lt;i</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S3.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. If there exist a distribution <math id="S3.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and a measurable set <math id="S3.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(A)\geq\frac{2}{\epsilon}\sum_{j&lt;i}2^{j-i}\mu_{j}^{\epsilon}(A)\quad\text{and}\quad\mu(A)\geq\epsilon," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(A)\geq\frac{2}{\epsilon}\sum_{j&lt;i}2^{j-i}\mu_{j}^{\epsilon}(A)\quad\text{and}\quad\mu(A)\geq\epsilon,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p10.2" class="ltx_p">we fix <math id="S3.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}^{\epsilon},A_{i}^{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}^{\epsilon},A_{i}^{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying this property. Otherwise, we end the recursive construction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose by contradiction that this construction does not terminate. Define <math id="S3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}^{\epsilon}:=A_{i}^{\epsilon}\setminus\bigcup_{j&gt;i}A_{j}^{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em">∖</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}^{\epsilon}:=A_{i}^{\epsilon}\setminus\bigcup_{j&gt;i}A_{j}^{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(B_{i}^{\epsilon})\geq\mu_{i}^{\epsilon}(B_{i}^{\epsilon})\geq\mu_{i}^{\epsilon}(A_{i}^{\epsilon})-\sum_{j&gt;i}\mu_{i}^{\epsilon}(A_{j}^{\epsilon})\geq\epsilon-\sum_{j&gt;i}\frac{\epsilon}{2^{j-i+1}}\mu_{j}^{\epsilon}(A_{j}^{\epsilon})\geq\frac{\epsilon}{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(B_{i}^{\epsilon})\geq\mu_{i}^{\epsilon}(B_{i}^{\epsilon})\geq\mu_{i}^{\epsilon}(A_{i}^{\epsilon})-\sum_{j&gt;i}\mu_{i}^{\epsilon}(A_{j}^{\epsilon})\geq\epsilon-\sum_{j&gt;i}\frac{\epsilon}{2^{j-i+1}}\mu_{j}^{\epsilon}(A_{j}^{\epsilon})\geq\frac{\epsilon}{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p11.2" class="ltx_p">Since the sets <math id="S3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}^{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}^{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> are disjoint, this contradicts the continuous submeasure assumption on <math id="S3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, we have <math id="S3.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\bigcap_{j\geq i}B_{i})\geq\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">⋂</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\bigcap_{j\geq i}B_{i})\geq\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math> while <math id="S3.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcap_{j\geq i}B_{j}\downarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⋂</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcap_{j\geq i}B_{j}\downarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>. As a result, the construction terminates. Let <math id="S3.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> be the last constructed index. Then, by construction, the finite measure <math id="S3.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{\epsilon}:=\frac{2}{\epsilon}\sum_{j\leq N_{\epsilon}}\mu_{j}^{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{\epsilon}:=\frac{2}{\epsilon}\sum_{j\leq N_{\epsilon}}\mu_{j}^{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies for any measurable set <math id="S3.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subseteq\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subseteq\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A)=\sup_{\mu\in\mathcal{U}}\mu(A)\leq\epsilon+\mu^{\epsilon}(A)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A)=\sup_{\mu\in\mathcal{U}}\mu(A)\leq\epsilon+\mu^{\epsilon}(A).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Now fix any positive decreasing sequence <math id="S3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon_{i})_{i\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon_{i})_{i\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{i\to\infty}\epsilon_{i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{i\to\infty}\epsilon_{i}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="S3.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S3.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{\epsilon_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{\epsilon_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite measure, we can write it as <math id="S3.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{\epsilon_{i}}=C_{i}\nu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{\epsilon_{i}}=C_{i}\nu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a probability measure and <math id="S3.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}=\mu^{\epsilon_{i}}(\mathcal{X})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}=\mu^{\epsilon_{i}}(\mathcal{X})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Without loss of generality, we may assume that <math id="S3.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C_{i})_{i\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C_{i})_{i\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> is non-decreasing while preserving <a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. In particular, the sequence formed by <math id="S3.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}:=\frac{\epsilon_{i}}{2^{i}C_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}:=\frac{\epsilon_{i}}{2^{i}C_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is non-increasing in <math id="S3.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. We then define the probability measure</p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}:=\sum_{i\geq 1}2^{-i}\nu_{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}:=\sum_{i\geq 1}2^{-i}\nu_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p12.2" class="ltx_p">and we fix a non-decreasing function <math id="S3.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:[0,1]\to\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:[0,1]\to\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> by setting <math id="S3.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(z)=2\epsilon_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(z)=2\epsilon_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in(\delta_{i+1},\delta_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in(\delta_{i+1},\delta_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.p12.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p12.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p12.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(z)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(z)=2</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.p12.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in(\delta_{1},1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in(\delta_{1},1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Now fix a measurable set <math id="S3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subseteq\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subseteq\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and denote <math id="S3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=\mu_{0}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=\mu_{0}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z&gt;\delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z&gt;\delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> then we immediately have <math id="S3.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A)\leq 1=\rho(\mu_{0}(A))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A)\leq 1=\rho(\mu_{0}(A))</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, if <math id="S3.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in(\delta_{i+1},\delta_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in(\delta_{i+1},\delta_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S3.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, from <a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> we have</p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A)\leq\epsilon_{i}+\mu^{\epsilon_{i}}(A)=\epsilon_{i}+C_{i}\nu_{i}(A)\overset{(i)}{\leq}\epsilon_{i}+C_{i}2^{i}\mu_{0}(A)\leq 2\epsilon_{i}=\rho(\mu_{0}(A))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A)\leq\epsilon_{i}+\mu^{\epsilon_{i}}(A)=\epsilon_{i}+C_{i}\nu_{i}(A)\overset{(i)}{\leq}\epsilon_{i}+C_{i}2^{i}\mu_{0}(A)\leq 2\epsilon_{i}=\rho(\mu_{0}(A)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p13.2" class="ltx_p">In <math id="S3.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i)</annotation></semantics></math> we used the definition of <math id="S3.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, if <math id="S3.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then similarly as above, <a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> implies that <math id="S3.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A)\leq\epsilon_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A)\leq\epsilon_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and hence <math id="S3.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A)=0=\rho(\mu_{0}(A))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A)=0=\rho(\mu_{0}(A))</annotation></semantics></math>. This ends the proof.
 <math id="S3.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\blacksquare" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\blacksquare</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Combining <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 1. ‣ 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemmas</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2. ‣ 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>
directly shows that any weakly threshold learnable family of distributions <math id="S3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is a generalized smoothed distribution class. It is further known that generalized smoothness classes are learnable for all VC classes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. This yields the following main characterization theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> be a family of distributions on <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. The following are equivalent:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is weakly threshold learnable.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is strongly VC learnable.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a generalized smoothed distribution class.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p15" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof  </span>We proved <span id="S3.p15.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) <math id="S3.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> (3)</span> in <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 1. ‣ 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemmas</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2. ‣ 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Specifically, if <math id="S3.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is weakly threshold-learnable, then <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 1. ‣ 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that <math id="S3.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a continuous submeasure and hence <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2. ‣ 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> exactly constructs a reference measure <math id="S3.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and a tolerance function <math id="S3.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="S3.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is generalized smooth. <span id="S3.p15.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) <math id="S3.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> (1)</span> is immediate. <span id="S3.p15.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3) <math id="S3.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> (2):</span> The learnability of all VC classes under generalized smoothed distribution classes was already observed in previous works, e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, Corollary 10]</cite>. We will further give concrete algorithms—<span id="S3.p15.1.5" class="ltx_text">ERM</span> and <span id="S3.p15.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">R-Cover</span>—that achieves VC-dimension-dependent regret bounds for any generalized smoothed distribution classes in the realizable and agnostic setting respectively within <a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 6. ‣ Realizable setting. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorems</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ Agnostic setting. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.
 <math id="S3.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\blacksquare" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\blacksquare</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Conceptually, <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows that any form of learnability on generalized thresholds—while being very simple VC=1 function classes—already forces strong structural properties on the distribution class <math id="S3.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, as captured by generalized smoothness. Perhaps more surprisingly, this shows that <em id="S3.p16.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">asymptotic</em> learnability guarantees on generalized thresholds imply <em id="S3.p16.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">non-asymptotic</em> regret guarantees on all VC classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p17.1" class="ltx_p">As a remark, this property may not hold for simpler classes than generalized thresholds. For instance, note that any countable function class <math id="S3.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is weakly learnable in the sense that there exists an algorithm which ensures sublinear excess regret compared to any fixed function within <math id="S3.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> even for unconstrained adversaries, e.g. see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, Lemma 34]</cite>. Hence, <math id="S3.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}=\{\text{all distributions on }\mathcal{X}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext>all distributions on </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}=\{\text{all distributions on }\mathcal{X}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is weakly learnable for any countable function class, but it is not learnable for any function class with infinite Littlestone dimension—including threshold function classes.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Uniform Covers</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Another perspective on generalized smoothness is through the lens of uniform covers.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 7</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Uniform Cover)<span id="Thmdefinition7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A distribution class <math id="Thmdefinition7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to admit <em id="Thmdefinition7.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">uniform covers</em> for a function class <math id="Thmdefinition7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> if for any <math id="Thmdefinition7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a finite set of functions <math id="Thmdefinition7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\epsilon}\subseteq\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\epsilon}\subseteq\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any <math id="Thmdefinition7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists <math id="Thmdefinition7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}\in\mathcal{F}_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}\in\mathcal{F}_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmdefinition7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\{x:f(x)\neq\hat{f}(x)\})\leq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\{x:f(x)\neq\hat{f}(x)\})\leq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is easy to see that generalized smoothness implies existence of uniform covers for all VC classes. Indeed, if <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is generalized smooth with respect to some reference measure <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and tolerance function <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, then for any <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, using a covering for <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> at scale <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=\rho^{-1}(\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=\rho^{-1}(\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> yields a uniform cover at scale <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for all distributions in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. We record this formally as <a href="#Thmtheorem20" title="Lemma 20 (Uniform Covers under Generalized Smoothness). ‣ F.2 Proof of ‣ Appendix F Proofs from ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A distribution class admitting uniform covers for a class <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is also sufficient for achieving low regret if the adversary is <em id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">oblivious</em> and the distribution class <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is known.
Indeed, as observed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, a standard Hedge algorithm run on the uniform cover <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> ensures regret at most <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon T+O(\sqrt{T\log|\mathcal{F}_{\epsilon}|})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon T+O(\sqrt{T\log|\mathcal{F}_{\epsilon}|})</annotation></semantics></math> against any oblivious <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>-constrained adversary.
But, to the best of our knowledge, there is no known direct way to extend this to the adaptive adversaries setting or the case when <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is unknown, without using the machinery of the coupling lemma introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> (which requires stronger assumption on the distribution class than just uniform covers).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A modification of our proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> actually shows that the existence of uniform covers for all VC classes is indeed equivalent to generalized smoothness.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> be a distribution class. Then <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> has uniform covers for all VC classes (equivalently, all generalized threshold classes) if and only if it is generalized smooth.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, our result can be seen as showing that if we insist on VC dependent regret bounds then uniform cover is also a necessary and sufficient condition for learnability.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Universal Algorithms</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the following two sections, we provide algorithms that achieve low regret under generalized smoothed distribution classes. As a reminder, as per the definitions of learnability in <a href="#Thmdefinition3" title="Definition 3 (Strong VC Learnability). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definitions</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2 (Weak VC Learnability). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, a distribution class <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is learnable if there exists a corresponding algorithm which achieves the desired learning guarantee. Importantly, the considered algorithm a priori requires knowledge of the distribution class (and the function class <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is unavoidable).
In this section, we instead focus on algorithms that do not require this prior knowledge on <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, yet still achieve learning under VC classes (or generalized thresholds) and any distribution class <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> for which this is achievable—equivalently, any generalized smoothed distribution class <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, by <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. We refer to such adaptive algorithms as <em id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">optimistically universal</em>, following the corresponding literature on “learning whenever possible” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. Note that their existence is not a priori guaranteed.<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          For instance, no optimistically universal algorithm exist for inductive supervised learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, or online adversarial contextual bandits <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To obtain universal algorithms with low regret under generalized smoothness, we leverage known results for smooth adversaries, which correspond to generalized smoothed classes with linear tolerance function <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\epsilon)=\epsilon/\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\epsilon)=\epsilon/\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, for smooth adversaries, Empirical Risk Minimization (ERM) already achieves sublinear regret for VC classes in the realizable setting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, while <span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">R-Cover</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>—an algorithm based on recursive empirical covers—is known to achieve sublinear regret for VC classes in the agnostic setting.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Coupling reduction lemma.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We then lift these guarantees to generalized smooth adversaries using a simple coupling reduction to smooth adversaries. Fix a generalized smoothed class <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> with base measure <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and tolerance <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that by definition, for any <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, any distribution places at most mass <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> on sets of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>-measure <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, distributions in <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> are intuitively <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon/\rho(\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon/\rho(\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth with respect to <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> up to a mass <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>. More formally, we can couple the sequence generated by a <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>-constrained adversary with a <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\epsilon/\rho(\epsilon))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\epsilon/\rho(\epsilon))</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth sequence which agree except on a fraction <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\rho(\epsilon))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\rho(\epsilon))</annotation></semantics></math> of the samples for which we replace the original sample with a dummy variable <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\emptyset}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\emptyset}</annotation></semantics></math>, as detailed below. This argument will allows us to import regret bounds from the smoothed online learning literature to the case of generalized smoothed adversaries.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For convenience, we say that a tolerance function <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:[0,1]\to\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:[0,1]\to\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">well-behaved</em> if it is non-decreasing, <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\epsilon\to 0}\rho(\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\epsilon\to 0}\rho(\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in(0,1]\mapsto\rho(\epsilon)/\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in(0,1]\mapsto\rho(\epsilon)/\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is non-increasing.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> be a generalized smoothed distribution class on <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> with a well-behaved tolerance function <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:[0,1]\to\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:[0,1]\to\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and a horizon <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. For convenience, fix a dummy new variable <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\emptyset}\notin\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\emptyset}\notin\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider any <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>-constrained adaptive adversary to generate samples <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="Thmtheorem5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a coupling of these samples with a sequence <math id="Thmtheorem5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{x}_{T}\in\mathcal{X}\cup\{x_{\emptyset}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{x}_{T}\in\mathcal{X}\cup\{x_{\emptyset}\}</annotation></semantics></math> which is generated from a <math id="Thmtheorem5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\epsilon}{2\rho(\epsilon)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\epsilon}{2\rho(\epsilon)}</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth adversary on <math id="Thmtheorem5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\cup\{x_{\emptyset}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\cup\{x_{\emptyset}\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="Thmtheorem5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{t}\in\{x_{t},x_{\emptyset}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{t}\in\{x_{t},x_{\emptyset}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}\mid\tilde{x}_{&lt;t},x_{&lt;t}]\leq\rho(\epsilon)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}\mid\tilde{x}_{&lt;t},x_{&lt;t}]\leq\rho(\epsilon).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As a remark, <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ Coupling reduction lemma. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> additionally assumes that the tolerance function <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is such that <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\epsilon)/\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\epsilon)/\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is non-increasing, which is a mild technical regularity condition. In fact, this property is automatic if distributions in <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> are non-atomic (see <a href="#Thmtheorem14" title="Proposition 14. ‣ B.3 Properties of Generalized Smoothness ‣ Appendix B Further preliminaries ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> for a formal statement and proof). More generally, note that this is without loss of generality: any generalized smoothed distribution class <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a concave tolerance function <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, up to replacing its original tolerance function with its concave envelope <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> (the smallest concave function <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\rho}(x)\geq\rho(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\rho}(x)\geq\rho(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>). In most scenarios, this will not change the achieved rates.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Realizable setting.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We start with the realizable setting, where there exists a function <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\star}\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\star}\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> fixed <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">a priori</em>, and such that <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}=f^{\star}(x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}=f^{\star}(x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this setting, we show that ERM universally learns VC function classes under any threshold learnable distribution class <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> be a function class with finite VC dimension and let <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> be a generalized smoothed distribution class on <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> with a well-behaved tolerance function <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:[0,1]\to\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:[0,1]\to\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, for any realizable <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>-constrained adversary, <span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ERM</span> makes predictions <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Reg}_{T}(\text{ERM})=\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(\hat{y}_{t})\right]\leq\inf_{\epsilon&gt;0}\left\{C\sqrt{\frac{\rho(\epsilon)}{\epsilon}dT\ln^{5}T}+\rho(\epsilon)T\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Reg</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ERM</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ln</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Reg}_{T}(\text{ERM})=\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(\hat{y}_{t})\right]\leq\inf_{\epsilon&gt;0}\left\{C\sqrt{\frac{\rho(\epsilon)}{\epsilon}dT\ln^{5}T}+\rho(\epsilon)T\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The proof essentially combines the coupling reduction <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ Coupling reduction lemma. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> with known regret bounds for ERM in the realizable setting. Naturally, for any <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, compared the bound for the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\epsilon/\rho(\epsilon))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\epsilon/\rho(\epsilon))</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth adversaries from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, Theorem 8]</cite>, the regret bound from <a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 6. ‣ Realizable setting. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> includes an additive term <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\epsilon)T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\epsilon)T</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the expected number of disagreements between the original and smoothed sequence constructed in <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ Coupling reduction lemma. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Agnostic setting.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we turn to the general agnostic setting in which no assumptions are made on the labels <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. We show that the algorithm <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">R-Cover</span> from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> also universally learns VC classes under any threshold learnable distribution class <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> be a function class with finite VC dimension and let <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> be a generalized smoothed distribution class on <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> with a well-behaved tolerance function <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:[0,1]\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:[0,1]\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, for any adaptive adversary, <span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">R-Cover</span> makes predictions <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(\hat{y}_{t})-\inf_{f\in\mathcal{F}}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(f(x_{t}))\right]\leq C\ln^{3/2}T\cdot\inf_{\epsilon&gt;0}\left\{\sqrt{\frac{\rho(\epsilon)}{\epsilon}dT}\cdot\ln T+\rho(\epsilon)T\sqrt{d}\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ln</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.055em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(\hat{y}_{t})-\inf_{f\in\mathcal{F}}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(f(x_{t}))\right]\leq C\ln^{3/2}T\cdot\inf_{\epsilon&gt;0}\left\{\sqrt{\frac{\rho(\epsilon)}{\epsilon}dT}\cdot\ln T+\rho(\epsilon)T\sqrt{d}\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for some universal constant <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Due to the recursive nature of <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">R-Cover</span>, the proof requires additional care to handle the disagreements between the original sequence <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and the smoothed sequence <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{x}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{x}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> from <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ Coupling reduction lemma. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Specifically, previous samples are used recursively to construct covers, hence disagreements between the two sequences may amplify with the depth of the construction. This increases the additive disagreement term in <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ Agnostic setting. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> by a factor <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{d}\cdot\text{polylog }T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>polylog </mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{d}\cdot\text{polylog }T</annotation></semantics></math>, compared to the ERM regret bound in <a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 6. ‣ Realizable setting. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Distribution-class-dependent regret bounds</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The previous sections established that generalized smoothness characterizes VC-dependent learnability and that universal algorithms exist which achieve low regret without knowing the specific distribution family <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. A natural follow-up question is: <em id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">can we do better when <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is known?</em></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When the distribution family <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is known to the learner, one can often obtain sharper regret bounds by exploiting additional quantitative structure of <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this section, we develop a simple combinatorial parameterization of distribution families and connect it to upper and lower regret bounds. The key insight is that the “complexity” of a distribution family can be measured by how fragmented mass can be: if <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> allows mass to be spread across many disjoint regions, learning is harder. This can be formalized by the <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-<em id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fragmentation number</em> of the distribution family <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, which intuitively measures how “fragmented” mass can be under distributions in <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> at scale <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 8</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Fragmentation number)<span id="Thmdefinition8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any distribution class <math id="Thmdefinition8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and scale <math id="Thmdefinition8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="Thmdefinition8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-fragmentation number of <math id="Thmdefinition8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined via</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon):=\max\{k\in\mathbb{N}:\exists\text{ disjoint }A_{1},\ldots,A_{k}\in\mathcal{B},\forall i\in[k],\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A_{i})\geq\epsilon\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mi>max</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> disjoint </mtext><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon):=\max\{k\in\mathbb{N}:\exists\text{ disjoint }A_{1},\ldots,A_{k}\in\mathcal{B},\forall i\in[k],\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A_{i})\geq\epsilon\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As a useful remark, <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a continuous submeasure if and only if <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> (see the beginning of the proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 1. ‣ 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for the proof).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In words, the <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-fragmentation number is maximum number of disjoint regions that can each carry at least <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> mass under some <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. Equivalently, it counts disjoint regions on which a <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>-constrained adversary may place <math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> mass. In particular, a <math id="S5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>-constrained adversary may emulate, for a fraction <math id="S5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> of the rounds, an adversary which selects the region on which the next sample lies, among these disjoint regions. This observation leads to the following regret lower bound for <math id="S5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>-constrained adversaries.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 8</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix a family of distributions <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3d\leq N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3d\leq N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, there exists a function class <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> of VC dimension at most <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any algorithm <span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">alg</span>,</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{Reg}_{T}(\text{alg};\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U})\gtrsim\sqrt{\epsilon dT\ln\frac{N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)}{d}}\wedge(\epsilon T)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Reg</mtext><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">alg</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≳</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{Reg}_{T}(\text{alg};\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U})\gtrsim\sqrt{\epsilon dT\ln\frac{N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)}{d}}\wedge(\epsilon T).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for some oblivious <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>-constrained adversary.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We next turn to regret upper bounds. We first give a quantitative version of <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2. ‣ 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> using fragmentation numbers. This essentially bounds the continuity modulus of the envelope functional <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> as a function of fragmentation numbers.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 9</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> be a family of distributions and <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, there exists a distribution <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any measurable set <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subseteq\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subseteq\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\epsilon}(A)\leq\frac{\epsilon}{N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)^{2}}\implies\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A)\leq 2\epsilon." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\epsilon}(A)\leq\frac{\epsilon}{N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)^{2}}\implies\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A)\leq 2\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The proof is constructive and largely similar to that of <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2. ‣ 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Intuitively, <a href="#Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 9. ‣ 5 Distribution-class-dependent regret bounds ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> shows that at the scale of events of mass <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>, the distribution class <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is essentially <math id="S5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth with respect to a fixed base distribution <math id="S5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>.
With this property at hand, we can run a simple Hedge algorithm over an appropriate cover of <math id="S5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, following standard agruments for smoothed adversaries <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, to achieve the following regret upper bound.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 10</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> be a family of distributions on <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. For any function class <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> of VC dimension <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an algorithm <span id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">alg</span> such that</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Reg}_{T}(\text{alg};\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U})\lesssim\inf_{\epsilon&gt;0}\{\sqrt{dT\ln(TN_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon))}+\epsilon T\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Reg</mtext><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">alg</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≲</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Reg}_{T}(\text{alg};\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U})\lesssim\inf_{\epsilon&gt;0}\{\sqrt{dT\ln(TN_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon))}+\epsilon T\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">As an important remark, both upper and lower bounds depend only on the logarithm of fragmentation numbers <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. In comparison, the bounds from <span id="S5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ERM</span> and <span id="S5.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">R-Cover</span> qualitatively depend <em id="S5.p7.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">polynomially</em> on the fragmentation numbers. Specifically, if <math id="S5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <a href="#Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 9. ‣ 5 Distribution-class-dependent regret bounds ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> suggests that <math id="S5.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is generalized smooth with a function <math id="S5.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\epsilon/N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)^{2})=2\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\epsilon/N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)^{2})=2\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Provided that <math id="S5.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is additionally concave, <a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 6. ‣ Realizable setting. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> yields the following regret bound for <span id="S5.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ERM</span>:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Reg}_{T}(\textsc{ERM};\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U})\lesssim\inf_{\epsilon&gt;0}\{N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)\sqrt{dT\ln^{5}T}+\epsilon T\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>Reg</mtext><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">ERM</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≲</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ln</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Reg}_{T}(\textsc{ERM};\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U})\lesssim\inf_{\epsilon&gt;0}\{N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)\sqrt{dT\ln^{5}T}+\epsilon T\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p7.2" class="ltx_p">for function classes <math id="S5.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> of VC dimension <math id="S5.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ Agnostic setting. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> yields similar regret bounds for <span id="S5.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">R-Cover</span>. Of course, this regret bound is looser than <a href="#Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 10. ‣ 5 Distribution-class-dependent regret bounds ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> but has the advantage of being achieved by a universal algorithm that does not require knowledge of the distribution class <math id="S5.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Differential Privacy</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we will extend the insights from our characterization of distributionally constrained online learning to the setting of differentially private learning.
Recall that differential privacy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> is a rigorous framework for ensuring that learning algorithms do not leak sensitive information about individual data points.
Though differential privacy can be defined is broad generality, we will focus on the setting of learning binary classifiers and present the definition specialized to this setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 9</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Differential Private Learning)<span id="Thmdefinition9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A randomized algorithm <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}:(\mathcal{X}\times\{0,1\})^{m}\to\left\{0,1\right\}^{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}:(\mathcal{X}\times\{0,1\})^{m}\to\left\{0,1\right\}^{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>-differentially private if for any pair of datasets <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S,S^{\prime}\in\mathcal{X}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S,S^{\prime}\in\mathcal{X}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> differing in a single entry, and for any measurable set <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\subseteq\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\subseteq\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S6.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{A}(S)\in E)\leq e^{\alpha}\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{A}(S^{\prime})\in E)+\beta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{A}(S)\in E)\leq e^{\alpha}\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{A}(S^{\prime})\in E)+\beta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition9.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition9.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Further, for a distribution class <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, a function class <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>-privately <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-accurately learnable under <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> with sample complexity <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> if there exists an <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>-differentially private algorithm <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}:\mathcal{X}^{m}\to\left\{0,1\right\}^{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}:\mathcal{X}^{m}\to\left\{0,1\right\}^{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any distribution <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\times\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\times\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> with marginal on <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S6.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{S\sim D^{m}}\left(L_{D}(\mathcal{A}(S))\leq\inf_{f\in\mathcal{F}}L_{D}(f)+\epsilon\right)\geq 1-\delta" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{S\sim D^{m}}\left(L_{D}(\mathcal{A}(S))\leq\inf_{f\in\mathcal{F}}L_{D}(f)+\epsilon\right)\geq 1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition9.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition9.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmdefinition9.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{D}(f)=\mathbb{P}_{(x,y)\sim D}(f(x)\neq y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{D}(f)=\mathbb{P}_{(x,y)\sim D}(f(x)\neq y)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With this definition at hand, we can now state our characterization of private learnability under distribution constraints.
This characterization mirrors our main result for online learning: generalized smoothness is necessary for private learnability with VC-dependent sample complexity.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 11</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is a constant <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that the following holds. Let <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> be a distribution class on <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any generalized threshold class <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(\epsilon,\mathcal{F})&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(\epsilon,\mathcal{F})&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math> such that for <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0.1,\frac{c_{0}}{m(\epsilon,\mathcal{F})^{2}\log m(\epsilon,\mathcal{F})})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0.1,\frac{c_{0}}{m(\epsilon,\mathcal{F})^{2}\log m(\epsilon,\mathcal{F})})</annotation></semantics></math>-privately <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,1/32)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,1/32)</annotation></semantics></math>-accurately learnable under <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> with sample complexity <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(\epsilon,\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(\epsilon,\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is a generalized smooth distribution class.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As a remark, we believe that a stronger version of this statement may be possible, by showing that generalized smoothness also characterizes private <em id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">asymptotic consistency</em>, for which accuracy is only required asymptotically for each fixed data distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We complement this result by showing that differentially private learnability with VC-dependent sample complexity is achievable under any generalized smooth distribution class.
In fact, this is a simple consequence of the fact that under generalized smoothness VC class has uniform covers (<a href="#Thmdefinition7" title="Definition 7 (Uniform Cover). ‣ 3.1 Uniform Covers ‣ 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) and the fact that for finite classes the exponential mechanism <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> provides private learnability with logarithmic dependence on the size of the class.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 12</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Private Learnability under Generalized Smoothness)<span id="Thmtheorem12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\rho,\mu_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\rho,\mu_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> generalized smooth distribution class on <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, for any function class <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> of VC dimension <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, any <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon,\delta\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon,\delta\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and any <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,0)</annotation></semantics></math>-differentially private algorithm that <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-accurately learns <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> with sample complexity</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\lesssim\frac{d\log(1/\epsilon)+\log(1/\delta)}{\epsilon^{2}}+\frac{d\log(\rho^{-1}(\epsilon))}{\alpha\epsilon}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≲</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\lesssim\frac{d\log(1/\epsilon)+\log(1/\delta)}{\epsilon^{2}}+\frac{d\log(\rho^{-1}(\epsilon))}{\alpha\epsilon}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This result can be seen as being related to the line of work on private learning with unlabelled public data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> since unlabelled public data from the base distribution suffices to get the improved sample complexity guarantees.
Further, using techniques from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> similar results can be obtained for query release and data release tasks under generalized smoothness assumptions.
It is an interesting research direction to understand whether oracle-efficient private learning algorithms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> can be obtained under generalized smoothness assumptions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have established that generalized smoothness exactly characterizes when VC-dimension-dependent regret bounds are achievable against distributionally constrained adversaries. This characterization bridges the gap between the classical <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth model and the fully adversarial setting, providing a complete picture of learnability under distribution shifts.
Further this characterization extends to the setting of differentially private learning, showing that generalized smoothness is necessary and sufficient for private learnability with VC-dependent sample complexity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our work fits into the broader program of understanding “beyond worst-case” models in online learning and private learning.
The characterization suggests that generalized smoothness is a fundamental structural property, much like how VC dimension and Littlestone dimension are fundamental for i.i.d. and adversarial learning respectively.
We hope this perspective inspires further work on understanding the landscape of learnability under various distributional assumptions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgments</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We acknowledge support from ARO through award W911NF-21-1-0328, Simons Foundation, and the NSF through awards DMS-2031883 and PHY-2019786, the DARPA AIQ program, and AFOSR FA9550-25-1-0375. Abhishek Shetty was supported by an NSF FODSI fellowship.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
