<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Proofs from <a href="#S3" title="3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a></h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We start by proving that for threshold learnable distribution classes <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, their envelope measure <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a continuous submeasure.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof  of <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 1. ‣ 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></span>
The first two properties of continuous submeasures are immediate to check by definition of <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}:=\sup_{\mu\in\mathcal{U}}\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></msub><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}:=\sup_{\mu\in\mathcal{U}}\mu</annotation></semantics></math>. We focus on proving the continuity property.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose by contradiction that <math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not continuous and fix a sequence of measurable sets <math id="A1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}\downarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}\downarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="A1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(B_{i})\geq 2\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(B_{i})\geq 2\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. From these decreasing sets, we construct by induction a sequence of disjoint measurable sets <math id="A1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A_{i})_{i\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A_{i})_{i\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for <math id="A1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="A1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A_{i})\geq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A_{i})\geq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, as follows. Let <math id="A1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{1}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{1}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. For a given <math id="A1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(B_{j_{i}})&gt;\frac{3}{2}\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(B_{j_{i}})&gt;\frac{3}{2}\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, which exists by construction of <math id="A1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B_{i})_{i\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B_{i})_{i\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{j}\downarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{j}\downarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists <math id="A1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{i+1}&gt;j_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{i+1}&gt;j_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(B_{j_{i}}\setminus B_{j_{i+1}})\geq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(B_{j_{i}}\setminus B_{j_{i+1}})\geq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. We then pose <math id="A1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}:=B_{j_{i}}\setminus B_{j_{i+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}:=B_{j_{i}}\setminus B_{j_{i+1}}</annotation></semantics></math>; in particular, <math id="A1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A_{i})\geq\mu(A_{i})\geq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A_{i})\geq\mu(A_{i})\geq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. By construction, <math id="A1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A_{i})_{i\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A_{i})_{i\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> are all disjoint, which ends the construction.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Fix any bijection <math id="A1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:\mathbb{Q}\to\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:\mathbb{Q}\to\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> between positive integers and rationals. For any rational <math id="A1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the function <math id="A1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{r}:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{r}:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the indicator of the set <math id="A1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{y\leq x,y\in\mathbb{Q}}A_{\phi(y)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{y\leq x,y\in\mathbb{Q}}A_{\phi(y)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We then consider the function class which collects of all such functions: <math id="A1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}:=\{f_{r}:r\in\mathbb{R}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}:=\{f_{r}:r\in\mathbb{R}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By construction, we can check that <math id="A1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is a generalized threshold function class since for any <math id="A1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\leq s\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leq s\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> one has <math id="A1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{r}\leq f_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{r}\leq f_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. For convenience, in the rest of the proof we use the following abuse of notation. For any interval <math id="A1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\subseteq\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\subseteq\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> we write <math id="A1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in I</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="A1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\bigcup_{r\in I\cap\mathbb{Q}}A_{\phi(r)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\bigcup_{r\in I\cap\mathbb{Q}}A_{\phi(r)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We now show that <math id="A1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is not weakly learnable for <math id="A1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. The corresponding construction follows classical arguments.
Specifically, we fix an online learner <span id="A1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">alg</span> and construct an instance on which they incur linear regret. To do so, we recursively a sequence of rationals <math id="A1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{t},b_{t},r_{t})_{t\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{t},b_{t},r_{t})_{t\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}&lt;b_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}&lt;b_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, and corresponding distributions <math id="A1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu_{t})_{t\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu_{t})_{t\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>. By default, initially we can pose <math id="A1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math>). Fix <math id="A1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and suppose <math id="A1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t-1}&lt;b_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t-1}&lt;b_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> have been constructed. We first pose</p>
<table id="A1.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{t}:=\frac{a_{t-1}+b_{t-1}}{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{t}:=\frac{a_{t-1}+b_{t-1}}{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">By construction, there exists <math id="A1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="A1.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}(A_{\phi(c_{t})})\geq\frac{\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A_{\phi(c_{t})})}{2}\geq\frac{\epsilon}{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}(A_{\phi(c_{t})})\geq\frac{\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A_{\phi(c_{t})})}{2}\geq\frac{\epsilon}{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p5.3" class="ltx_p">Next, we consider the online learning setup for which <math id="A1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are sampled independently from <math id="A1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1},\ldots,\mu_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1},\ldots,\mu_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively and consider the values <math id="A1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t^{\prime}}=f_{c_{t}}(x_{t^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t^{\prime}}=f_{c_{t}}(x_{t^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}&lt;t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}&lt;t</annotation></semantics></math>. We now define the value</p>
<table id="A1.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}:=\arg\min_{z\in\{0,1\}}\mathbb{P}(\hat{y}_{t}^{(t)}=z,x_{t}\in\{c_{t}\})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}:=\arg\min_{z\in\{0,1\}}\mathbb{P}(\hat{y}_{t}^{(t)}=z,x_{t}\in\{c_{t}\}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p5.4" class="ltx_p">where we denoted by <math id="A1.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{t}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{t}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> the prediction of the algorithm at time <math id="A1.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> for this online learning instance.
If <math id="A1.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=0</annotation></semantics></math> we pose <math id="A1.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{t}=c_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{t}=c_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and fix <math id="A1.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}\in(a_{t-1},c_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}\in(a_{t-1},c_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="A1.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{x_{t^{\prime}}\sim\mu_{t^{\prime}}}\left(\exists t^{\prime}\in[t]:x_{t^{\prime}}\in(a_{t},b_{t})\right)\leq\epsilon/8." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{x_{t^{\prime}}\sim\mu_{t^{\prime}}}\left(\exists t^{\prime}\in[t]:x_{t^{\prime}}\in(a_{t},b_{t})\right)\leq\epsilon/8.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p5.5" class="ltx_p">Note that this is indeed possible since as <math id="A1.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}\to c_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}\to c_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the subset of <math id="A1.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to interval <math id="A1.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{t},c_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{t},c_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> decreases to the empty set. Conversely, if <math id="A1.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, we pose <math id="A1.p5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}=c_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}=c_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and fix any <math id="A1.p5.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{t}\in(c_{t},b_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{t}\in(c_{t},b_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <a href="#A1.E7" title="In Appendix A Proofs from ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> holds. This ends the recursive construction of the sequences. By construction, note that <math id="A1.p5.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{t})_{t\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{t})_{t\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p5.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(b_{t})_{t\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(b_{t})_{t\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> are convergent to the same real value which we denote <math id="A1.p5.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\star}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\star}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We now check that for the sequence of instances <math id="A1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu_{t})_{t\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu_{t})_{t\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> and function <math id="A1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\star}:=f_{r^{\star}}\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\star}:=f_{r^{\star}}\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, the learner induces linear regret. By construction, note that <math id="A1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> has value <math id="A1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> on the set <math id="A1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{c_{t}\}=A_{\phi(c_{t})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{c_{t}\}=A_{\phi(c_{t})}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, for any <math id="A1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A1.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}[\hat{y}_{t}\neq f^{\star}(x_{t})]\right]\geq\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbb{P}(\hat{y}_{t}=z_{t},x_{t}\in\{c_{t}\})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≥</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}[\hat{y}_{t}\neq f^{\star}(x_{t})]\right]\geq\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbb{P}(\hat{y}_{t}=z_{t},x_{t}\in\{c_{t}\}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Note that by construction we have <math id="A1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{t},r^{\star}\in(a_{t-1},b_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{t},r^{\star}\in(a_{t-1},b_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, <math id="A1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{c_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{c_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> yield the same values <math id="A1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}&lt;t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}&lt;t</annotation></semantics></math> whenever for all previous samples <math id="A1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}&lt;t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}&lt;t</annotation></semantics></math> one has <math id="A1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t^{\prime}}\notin(a_{t-1},b_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t^{\prime}}\notin(a_{t-1},b_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, for any <math id="A1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A1.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(\hat{y}_{t}=z_{t},x_{t}\in\{c_{t}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(\hat{y}_{t}=z_{t},x_{t}\in\{c_{t}\})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex22.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\mathbb{P}(\hat{y}_{t}^{(t)}=z_{t},x_{t}\in\{c_{t}\})-\mathbb{P}(\exists t^{\prime}\in[t-1]:x_{t^{\prime}}\in(a_{t-1},b_{t-1}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\mathbb{P}(\hat{y}_{t}^{(t)}=z_{t},x_{t}\in\{c_{t}\})-\mathbb{P}(\exists t^{\prime}\in[t-1]:x_{t^{\prime}}\in(a_{t-1},b_{t-1}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overset{(i)}{\geq}\frac{\mathbb{P}(x_{t}\in\{c_{t}\})}{2}-\frac{\epsilon}{8}\overset{(ii)}{\geq}\frac{\epsilon}{8}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overset{(i)}{\geq}\frac{\mathbb{P}(x_{t}\in\{c_{t}\})}{2}-\frac{\epsilon}{8}\overset{(ii)}{\geq}\frac{\epsilon}{8}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p6.3" class="ltx_p">In <math id="A1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i)</annotation></semantics></math> we used <a href="#A1.E6" title="In Appendix A Proofs from ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eqs.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and <a href="#A1.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ Appendix A Proofs from ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and in <math id="A1.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ii)</annotation></semantics></math> we used <a href="#A1.E5" title="In Appendix A Proofs from ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Plugging these estimates within the previous equation shows that for all <math id="A1.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A1.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}[\hat{y}_{t}\neq f^{\star}(x_{t})]\right]\geq\frac{\epsilon T}{8}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}[\hat{y}_{t}\neq f^{\star}(x_{t})]\right]\geq\frac{\epsilon T}{8}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p6.4" class="ltx_p">This ends the proof that <math id="A1.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is not weakly learnable for <math id="A1.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, reaching a contradiction. Hence <math id="A1.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a continuous submeasure.
 <math id="A1.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\blacksquare" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\blacksquare</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Further preliminaries</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Additional definitions</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We give the definition of the Littlestone dimension below, which is known to characterize learnable function classes for fully unconstrained adversaries <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 10</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Littlestone Dimension)<span id="Thmdefinition10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <em id="Thmdefinition10.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">Littlestone dimension</em> of a function class <math id="Thmdefinition10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="Thmdefinition10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Ldim}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ldim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Ldim}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math>, is the largest integer <math id="Thmdefinition10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> such that there exists a complete binary tree of depth <math id="Thmdefinition10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> with internal nodes labeled by elements of <math id="Thmdefinition10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and edges labeled by <math id="Thmdefinition10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that for every root-to-leaf path <math id="Thmdefinition10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},b_{1}),\ldots,(x_{d},b_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},b_{1}),\ldots,(x_{d},b_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a function <math id="Thmdefinition10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="Thmdefinition10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x_{i})=b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x_{i})=b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmdefinition10.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[d]</annotation></semantics></math>. If such trees exist for arbitrarily large <math id="Thmdefinition10.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, then the Littlestone dimension is infinite.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Concentration inequlities</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Freedman’s inequality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> gives tail probability bounds for martingales. The statement below is for instance taken from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, Theorem 1]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Lemma 9]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 13</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Freedman’s inequality)<span id="Thmtheorem13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{t})_{t\in T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{t})_{t\in T}</annotation></semantics></math> be a real-valued martingale difference sequence adapted to filtration <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{t})_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{t})_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|Z_{t}|\leq R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|Z_{t}|\leq R</annotation></semantics></math> almost surely, then for any <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in(0,1/R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in(0,1/R)</annotation></semantics></math>, with probability at least <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{t=1}^{T}Z_{t}\leq\eta\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbb{E}[Z_{t}^{2}\mid\mathcal{F}_{t-1}]+\frac{\ln 1/\delta}{\eta}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mi>η</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{t=1}^{T}Z_{t}\leq\eta\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbb{E}[Z_{t}^{2}\mid\mathcal{F}_{t-1}]+\frac{\ln 1/\delta}{\eta}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Properties of Generalized Smoothness</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we give further properties of generalized smoothness, starting with short proof of the equivalence between generalized smoothness and having uniform covers for all VC classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof  of <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Proposition 4. ‣ 3.1 Uniform Covers ‣ 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a></span>
We first check that a <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu_{0},\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu_{0},\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>-generalized smooth distribution class <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> has uniform covers for any VC class <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. The distribution <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a finite cover <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> at scale <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta:=\rho^{-1}(\epsilon)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta:=\rho^{-1}(\epsilon)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, that is, for any <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> there exists <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}\in\mathcal{F}_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}\in\mathcal{F}_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}(\{x:f(x)\neq\hat{f}(x)\})\leq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}(\{x:f(x)\neq\hat{f}(x)\})\leq\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. By the generalized smoothness property, <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is therefore a uniform cover for <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> for scale <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To complete the proof, it suffices to check that if <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> has uniform covers for all generalized thresholds, then it is generalized smooth. Indeed, if it doesn’t hold, <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2. ‣ 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a continuous submeasure. Following the standard argument in the beginning of the proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 1. ‣ 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, this shows that there exists <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and a sequence of disjoint measurable sets <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A_{i})_{i\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A_{i})_{i\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A_{i})\geq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A_{i})\geq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. We then construct the generalized threshold class <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{F}:=\{x\mapsto\mathbbm{1}[x\in\bigcup_{j\geq i}A_{j}]:i\geq 1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}:=\{x\mapsto\mathbbm{1}[x\in\bigcup_{j\geq i}A_{j}]:i\geq 1\}</annotation></semantics></math> which by construction is an infinite <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-packing for <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>. That is, for any <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\neq f^{\prime}\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\neq f^{\prime}\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\{x:f(x)\neq f^{\prime}(x)\})\geq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\{x:f(x)\neq f^{\prime}(x)\})\geq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> does not admit finite uniform covers for scale <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math>, ending the proof.
 <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\blacksquare" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\blacksquare</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we show that the tolerance function <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> for a <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu_{0},\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu_{0},\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>-generalized smooth distribution class <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is necessarily well-behaved if all distribution in <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> are non-atomic.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 14</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu_{0},\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu_{0},\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>-generalized smooth distribution class where <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:[0,1]\to\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:[0,1]\to\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> is non-decreasing and <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\epsilon\to 0}\rho(\epsilon)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\epsilon\to 0}\rho(\epsilon)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, such that all <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> are non-atomic (i.e., <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(A)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(A)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all atoms <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>). Then, the function <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> defined via</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{\rho}(\epsilon)=\sup\{\mu(A):\mu\in\mathcal{U},A\in\mathcal{B},\mu_{0}(A)\leq\epsilon\},\quad\epsilon\geq 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\rho}(\epsilon)=\sup\{\mu(A):\mu\in\mathcal{U},A\in\mathcal{B},\mu_{0}(A)\leq\epsilon\},\quad\epsilon\geq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem14.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem14.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is well-behaved and <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu_{0},\tilde{\rho})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu_{0},\tilde{\rho})</annotation></semantics></math>-generalized smooth. Also, <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\rho}(\epsilon)\leq\rho(\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\rho}(\epsilon)\leq\rho(\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof  </span>First, since <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu_{0},\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu_{0},\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>-generalized smooth, for any <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}(A)\leq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}(A)\leq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(A)\leq\rho(\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(A)\leq\rho(\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, by construction of <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\rho}(\epsilon)\leq\rho(\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\rho}(\epsilon)\leq\rho(\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>. Also, by construction we directly have <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\rho}(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\rho}(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">It remains to check that <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> is well-behaved. To do so, we first show that for any non-atomic distribution <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\ll\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>≪</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\ll\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in[0,\mu_{0}(A)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in[0,\mu_{0}(A)]</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\subseteq A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\subseteq A</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}(B)\leq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}(B)\leq\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(B)\geq\mu(A)\frac{\delta}{\mu_{0}(A)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(B)\geq\mu(A)\frac{\delta}{\mu_{0}(A)}</annotation></semantics></math>. To do so, for any <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> we define the set <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{z}:=A\cap\{x:\frac{d\mu}{d\mu_{0}}(x)\geq z\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{z}:=A\cap\{x:\frac{d\mu}{d\mu_{0}}(x)\geq z\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\ll\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>≪</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\ll\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{z}\downarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{z}\downarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}=A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}=A</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, there exists <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\delta}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\delta}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}(B_{z_{\delta}})\geq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}(B_{z_{\delta}})\geq\delta</annotation></semantics></math> but <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}(A\cap\{x:\frac{d\mu}{d\mu_{0}}(x)&gt;z_{\delta}\})\leq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}(A\cap\{x:\frac{d\mu}{d\mu_{0}}(x)&gt;z_{\delta}\})\leq\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. As a result, there exists a set <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="A2.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\cap\left\{x:\frac{d\mu}{d\mu_{0}}(x)&gt;z_{\delta}\right\}\subseteq B\subseteq A\cap\left\{x:\frac{d\mu}{d\mu_{0}}(x)\geq z_{\delta}\right\}=B_{z_{\delta}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\cap\left\{x:\frac{d\mu}{d\mu_{0}}(x)&gt;z_{\delta}\right\}\subseteq B\subseteq A\cap\left\{x:\frac{d\mu}{d\mu_{0}}(x)\geq z_{\delta}\right\}=B_{z_{\delta}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">and an atom <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\cup C\subseteq B_{z_{\delta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\cup C\subseteq B_{z_{\delta}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}(B)\leq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}(B)\leq\delta</annotation></semantics></math> but <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}(B\cup C)\geq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}(B\cup C)\geq\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Now we compute</p>
<table id="A2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mu(A)=\mu(B\cup C)+\mu(A\setminus(B\cup C))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mu(A)=\mu(B\cup C)+\mu(A\setminus(B\cup C))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overset{(i)}{\leq}\mu(B\cup C)+z_{\delta}\mu_{0}(A\setminus(B\cup C))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overset{(i)}{\leq}\mu(B\cup C)+z_{\delta}\mu_{0}(A\setminus(B\cup C))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overset{(ii)}{\leq}\mu(B\cup C)+\frac{\mu(B\cup C)}{\mu_{0}(B\cup C)}\mu_{0}(A\setminus(B\cup C))\overset{(iii)}{=}\mu(B)\frac{\mu_{0}(A)}{\mu_{0}(B\cup C)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overset{(ii)}{\leq}\mu(B\cup C)+\frac{\mu(B\cup C)}{\mu_{0}(B\cup C)}\mu_{0}(A\setminus(B\cup C))\overset{(iii)}{=}\mu(B)\frac{\mu_{0}(A)}{\mu_{0}(B\cup C)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p6.3" class="ltx_p">In <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i)</annotation></semantics></math> we used the fact that <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\setminus(B\cup C)\subseteq\{x:\frac{d\mu}{d\mu_{0}}(x)\leq z_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\setminus(B\cup C)\subseteq\{x:\frac{d\mu}{d\mu_{0}}(x)\leq z_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and in <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ii)</annotation></semantics></math> we used <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\cup C\subseteq B_{z_{\delta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\cup C\subseteq B_{z_{\delta}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(iii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(iii)</annotation></semantics></math> we used <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(B)=\mu(B\cup C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(B)=\mu(B\cup C)</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is non-atomic. This exactly gives the desired bound <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(B)\geq\mu(A)\frac{\delta}{\mu_{0}(A)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(B)\geq\mu(A)\frac{\delta}{\mu_{0}(A)}</annotation></semantics></math> while <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}(B)\leq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}(B)\leq\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">With this property at hand, we can check that <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\rho}(\epsilon)/\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\rho}(\epsilon)/\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is non-increasing in <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. First, note that all <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\ll\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>≪</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\ll\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise we would have <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\epsilon\to 0}\rho(\epsilon)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\epsilon\to 0}\rho(\epsilon)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Also, by assumption, all distributions <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> are non-atomic. Now fix any <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\epsilon_{1}\leq\epsilon_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\epsilon_{1}\leq\epsilon_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> there is <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}(A)\leq\epsilon_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}(A)\leq\epsilon_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(A)\geq\tilde{\rho}(\epsilon_{2})-\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(A)\geq\tilde{\rho}(\epsilon_{2})-\eta</annotation></semantics></math>. From the previous paragraph, there is <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\subseteq A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\subseteq A</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}(B)\leq\epsilon_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}(B)\leq\epsilon_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(B)\geq(\tilde{\rho}(\epsilon_{2})-\eta)\frac{\epsilon_{1}}{\epsilon_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(B)\geq(\tilde{\rho}(\epsilon_{2})-\eta)\frac{\epsilon_{1}}{\epsilon_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This holds for any <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and hence <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\rho}(\epsilon_{1})\geq\tilde{\rho}(\epsilon_{2})\frac{\epsilon_{1}}{\epsilon_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\rho}(\epsilon_{1})\geq\tilde{\rho}(\epsilon_{2})\frac{\epsilon_{1}}{\epsilon_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
 <math id="A2.SS3.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\blacksquare" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\blacksquare</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Proofs from <a href="#S4" title="4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a></h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We start by proving the reduction coupling <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ Coupling reduction lemma. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> from generalized smooth adversaries to (standard) smooth adversaries.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof  of <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ Coupling reduction lemma. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a></span>
Denote by <math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> the base measure for the generalized smoothed class <math id="A3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. We also denote by <math id="A3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mu}_{0}:=\frac{1}{2}\mu_{0}+\frac{1}{2}\delta_{x_{\emptyset}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mu}_{0}:=\frac{1}{2}\mu_{0}+\frac{1}{2}\delta_{x_{\emptyset}}</annotation></semantics></math> the mixture of the base measure and a Dirac at the dummy variable <math id="A3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\emptyset}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\emptyset}</annotation></semantics></math>. For convenience, we denote <math id="A3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta:=\rho(\epsilon)/\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta:=\rho(\epsilon)/\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any <math id="A3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math>, denote by <math id="A3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> the distribution of <math id="A3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> conditional on the past history <math id="A3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{&lt;t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{&lt;t}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then construct the sample <math id="A3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> via</p>
<table id="A3.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{t}:=\begin{cases}x_{t}&amp;\text{if }\frac{d\mu_{t}}{d\mu_{0}}(x_{t})\leq\eta\\
x_{\emptyset}&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{t}:=\begin{cases}x_{t}&amp;\text{if }\frac{d\mu_{t}}{d\mu_{0}}(x_{t})\leq\eta\\
x_{\emptyset}&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="A3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mu}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mu}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> the distribution of <math id="A3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> conditional to <math id="A3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{&lt;t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{&lt;t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We can immediately check that for any <math id="A3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A3.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\frac{d\tilde{\mu}_{t}}{d\tilde{\mu}_{0}}(x)\leq 2\frac{d\tilde{\mu}_{t}}{d\mu_{0}}(x)=2\frac{d\mu_{t}}{d\mu_{0}}(x)\mathbbm{1}\left[\frac{d\mu_{t}}{d\mu_{0}}(x)\leq\eta\right]\leq 2\eta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d\tilde{\mu}_{t}}{d\tilde{\mu}_{0}}(x)\leq 2\frac{d\tilde{\mu}_{t}}{d\mu_{0}}(x)=2\frac{d\mu_{t}}{d\mu_{0}}(x)\mathbbm{1}\left[\frac{d\mu_{t}}{d\mu_{0}}(x)\leq\eta\right]\leq 2\eta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, we clearly have <math id="A3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d\tilde{\mu}_{t}}{d\tilde{\mu}_{0}}(x_{\emptyset})\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d\tilde{\mu}_{t}}{d\tilde{\mu}_{0}}(x_{\emptyset})\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="A3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mu}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mu}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> places <math id="A3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/2</annotation></semantics></math> mass onto <math id="A3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\emptyset}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\emptyset}</annotation></semantics></math>. This ends the proof that <math id="A3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{x}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{x}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth with respect to <math id="A3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mu}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mu}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, for any <math id="A3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}\mid\tilde{x}_{&lt;t},x_{&lt;t}]=\mathbb{P}\left[\frac{d\mu_{t}}{d\mu_{0}}(x_{t})&gt;\eta\mid x_{&lt;t}\right]=\mu_{t}\left(\left\{\frac{d\mu_{t}}{d\mu_{0}}(x)&gt;\eta\right\}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}\mid\tilde{x}_{&lt;t},x_{&lt;t}]=\mathbb{P}\left[\frac{d\mu_{t}}{d\mu_{0}}(x_{t})&gt;\eta\mid x_{&lt;t}\right]=\mu_{t}\left(\left\{\frac{d\mu_{t}}{d\mu_{0}}(x)&gt;\eta\right\}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">For convenience, denote <math id="A3.p3.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A_{t}:=\{\frac{d\mu_{t}}{d\mu_{0}}(x)&gt;\eta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{t}:=\{\frac{d\mu_{t}}{d\mu_{0}}(x)&gt;\eta\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, by construction, <math id="A3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\mu_{0}(A_{t})&lt;\mu_{t}(A_{t})\leq\rho(\mu_{0}(A_{t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\mu_{0}(A_{t})&lt;\mu_{t}(A_{t})\leq\rho(\mu_{0}(A_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math>,
where in the last inequality we used <math id="A3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and the generalized smoothed property.
Recalling that <math id="A3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=\frac{\rho(\epsilon)}{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=\frac{\rho(\epsilon)}{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\mapsto\frac{\rho(\alpha)}{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\mapsto\frac{\rho(\alpha)}{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is non-increasing, we obtain <math id="A3.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}(A_{t})\leq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}(A_{t})\leq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and in turn, <math id="A3.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}(A_{t})\leq\rho(\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}(A_{t})\leq\rho(\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>. This ends the proof.
 <math id="A3.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\blacksquare" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\blacksquare</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we prove <a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 6. ‣ Realizable setting. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> which gives regret bounds for ERM under any VC class and generalized smooth distribution class <math id="A3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof  of <a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 6. ‣ Realizable setting. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a></span>
We will use <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ Coupling reduction lemma. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> to reduce the problem to smooth adversaries, for which <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> gives regret lower bounds. To do so, we fix <math id="A3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and letting <math id="A3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:=\frac{\epsilon}{2\rho(\epsilon)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:=\frac{\epsilon}{2\rho(\epsilon)}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ Coupling reduction lemma. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> to construct the <math id="A3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth sequence <math id="A3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{x}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{x}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> as guaranteed by this result. We then extend the function class <math id="A3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\cup\{x_{\emptyset}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\cup\{x_{\emptyset}\}</annotation></semantics></math> by posing <math id="A3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x_{\emptyset})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x_{\emptyset})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. Throughout, we use tildes to distinguish between quantities computed for the smooth sequence <math id="A3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math> or the original sequence <math id="A3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="A3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> the prediction function chosen by the ERM algorithm at time <math id="A3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> (which was trained on the available data <math id="A3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{t^{\prime}},f^{\star}(x_{t^{\prime}}))_{t^{\prime}&lt;t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{t^{\prime}},f^{\star}(x_{t^{\prime}}))_{t^{\prime}&lt;t}</annotation></semantics></math>).
The main observation is that <math id="A3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> would still be an ERM prediction function on the training data <math id="A3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tilde{x}_{t^{\prime}},f^{\star}(\tilde{x}_{t^{\prime}}))_{t^{\prime}&lt;t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{x}_{t^{\prime}},f^{\star}(\tilde{x}_{t^{\prime}}))_{t^{\prime}&lt;t}</annotation></semantics></math> by construction of <math id="A3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{&lt;t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{&lt;t}</annotation></semantics></math> and since all functions coincide on <math id="A3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\emptyset}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\emptyset}</annotation></semantics></math>. For convenience, denote <math id="A3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{y}_{t}:=f_{t}(\tilde{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{y}_{t}:=f_{t}(\tilde{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the sequence <math id="A3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth, the regret bound from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, Theorem 8]</cite> applies:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{y}_{t}\neq f^{\star}(\tilde{x}_{t})]\right]\leq C\sqrt{\frac{d}{\sigma}T\ln^{5}T}=C\sqrt{\frac{2\rho(\epsilon)}{\epsilon}dT\ln^{5}T}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>σ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ln</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ln</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{y}_{t}\neq f^{\star}(\tilde{x}_{t})]\right]\leq C\sqrt{\frac{d}{\sigma}T\ln^{5}T}=C\sqrt{\frac{2\rho(\epsilon)}{\epsilon}dT\ln^{5}T}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">for some constant <math id="A3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
As a slight note, the <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, Theorem 8]</cite> gives a dependency of the regret in <math id="A3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, but this can be improved to <math id="A3.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\sqrt{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>σ</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\sqrt{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> with the same proof as noted in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. As a result, we obtain</p>
<table id="A3.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(\hat{y}_{t})\right]\leq\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{y}_{t}\neq f^{\star}(\tilde{x}_{t})]+\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}]\right]\leq C\sqrt{\frac{2\rho(\epsilon)}{\epsilon}dT\ln^{5}T}+\rho(\epsilon)T," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ln</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(\hat{y}_{t})\right]\leq\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{y}_{t}\neq f^{\star}(\tilde{x}_{t})]+\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}]\right]\leq C\sqrt{\frac{2\rho(\epsilon)}{\epsilon}dT\ln^{5}T}+\rho(\epsilon)T,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p6.3" class="ltx_p">where in the last inequality we used the last property from <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ Coupling reduction lemma. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Taking the infimum over <math id="A3.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> ends the proof.
 <math id="A3.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\blacksquare" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\blacksquare</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we prove the regret bound on <span id="A3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">R-Cover</span> from <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ Agnostic setting. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> for agnostic distributionally constrained adversaries.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof  of <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ Agnostic setting. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a></span>
Again, we use <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ Coupling reduction lemma. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> to reduce the problem to smooth adversaries, for which <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> gives regret lower bounds. We use the same notations for the <math id="A3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:=\frac{\epsilon}{2\rho(\epsilon)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:=\frac{\epsilon}{2\rho(\epsilon)}</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth sequence <math id="A3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{x}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{x}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> as in the proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 6. ‣ Realizable setting. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Importantly, the only part of the original proof which uses smoothness is in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Lemma 11]</cite> which bounds some discrepancy terms denoted <math id="A3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{k}^{(p,r)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{k}^{(p,r)}</annotation></semantics></math> (for both classification and regression), and when going from oblivious regret bounds to adaptive regret bounds (only for classification). In particular, their regret decomposition still holds even for the regression setup.
We rewrite these bounds here for completeness. For context, the <span id="A3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">R-Cover</span> algorithm proceeds by epochs on several layers <math id="A3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\{0,\ldots,P=\left\lfloor\log_{2}T\right\rfloor\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\{0,\ldots,P=\left\lfloor\log_{2}T\right\rfloor\}</annotation></semantics></math>: these layer-<math id="A3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> epochs are denoted <math id="A3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}^{(p)},\ldots,E_{N_{p}}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}^{(p)},\ldots,E_{N_{p}}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math> and form a roughly-balanced partition of <math id="A3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[T]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{p}=2^{P-p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{p}=2^{P-p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Their end points are denoted <math id="A3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{k}^{(p)}:=(T_{k-1}^{(p)},T_{k}^{(p)}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{k}^{(p)}:=(T_{k-1}^{(p)},T_{k}^{(p)}]</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A3.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in[N_{p}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in[N_{p}]</annotation></semantics></math>. As a first step of the original proof (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Equation (14)]</cite>, the regret of the learning-with-experts algorithm at layer <math id="A3.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> during epoch <math id="A3.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{k}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{k}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A3.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in[N_{p}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in[N_{p}]</annotation></semantics></math> are bounded using the following quantity (with simplified notation)</p>
<table id="A3.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{k}^{(p)}:=\sum_{t\in E_{p}^{(k)}}\mathbb{E}_{r\sim\hat{p}_{t}}\left[(\ell_{t}(\hat{y}_{t})-\ell_{t}(\mathcal{A}_{r}(t)))^{2}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{k}^{(p)}:=\sum_{t\in E_{p}^{(k)}}\mathbb{E}_{r\sim\hat{p}_{t}}\left[(\ell_{t}(\hat{y}_{t})-\ell_{t}(\mathcal{A}_{r}(t)))^{2}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p9.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="A3.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the distribution among experts used by the algorithm at time <math id="A3.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the prediction of the expert, and <math id="A3.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{l,r}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{l,r}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the prediction of expert <math id="A3.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="A3.p9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. The main point is that we can bound</p>
<table id="A3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Delta_{k}^{(p)}\leq\bar{\Delta}_{k}^{(p)}+\sum_{t\in E_{p}^{(k)}}\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}]\quad\text{where}\quad\bar{\Delta}_{k}^{(p)}:=\sum_{t\in E_{p}^{(k)}}\mathbb{E}_{r\sim\hat{p}_{t}}\left[(\ell_{t}(\hat{y}_{t})-\ell_{t}(\mathcal{A}_{r}(t)))^{2}\right]\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{t}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></munder><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mtext>where</mtext><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{k}^{(p)}\leq\bar{\Delta}_{k}^{(p)}+\sum_{t\in E_{p}^{(k)}}\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}]\quad\text{where}\quad\bar{\Delta}_{k}^{(p)}:=\sum_{t\in E_{p}^{(k)}}\mathbb{E}_{r\sim\hat{p}_{t}}\left[(\ell_{t}(\hat{y}_{t})-\ell_{t}(\mathcal{A}_{r}(t)))^{2}\right]\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{t}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p9.3" class="ltx_p">As a note, <math id="A3.p9.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\Delta}_{k}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\Delta}_{k}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math> is distinct from the quantity <math id="A3.p9.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Delta}_{k}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Delta}_{k}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math> which would correspond to using the smoothed sequence <math id="A3.p9.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> throughout the game instead of <math id="A3.p9.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, since <span id="A3.p9.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">R-Cover</span> constructs covers (which affect <math id="A3.p9.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>) using the available data <math id="A3.p9.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similarly, we can bound the following quantity which is also used within the original proof:</p>
<table id="A3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Lambda_{k}^{(p)}:=\sum_{t\in E_{k}^{(p)}}\ell_{t}\left(f_{k,S}^{(p)}(x_{t})\right)-\ell_{t}(f^{\star}(x_{t}))\leq\tilde{\Lambda}_{k}^{(p)}+\sum_{t\in E_{p}^{(k)}}\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></munder><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></munder><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{k}^{(p)}:=\sum_{t\in E_{k}^{(p)}}\ell_{t}\left(f_{k,S}^{(p)}(x_{t})\right)-\ell_{t}(f^{\star}(x_{t}))\leq\tilde{\Lambda}_{k}^{(p)}+\sum_{t\in E_{p}^{(k)}}\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p9.4" class="ltx_p">where we recall that <math id="A3.p9.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Lambda}_{k}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Lambda}_{k}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the quantity to <math id="A3.p9.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{k}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{k}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math> but for the smoothed sequence <math id="A3.p9.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">We further the bounds as in the original proof, using <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Lemma 10]</cite>. Formally, we first define</p>
<table id="A3.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{k}^{(p)}:=\{f\in\mathcal{F}:\max_{t\in[T_{k-1}^{(p)}]}\left|f(x_{t})-f^{\star}(x_{t})\right|\leq 2\alpha\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{k}^{(p)}:=\{f\in\mathcal{F}:\max_{t\in[T_{k-1}^{(p)}]}\left|f(x_{t})-f^{\star}(x_{t})\right|\leq 2\alpha\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p10.2" class="ltx_p">which corresponds to the set of functions approximately consistent with <math id="A3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> on instances from previous layer-<math id="A3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> epochs. Then, for any <math id="A3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we define</p>
<table id="A3.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bar{\Gamma}_{k}^{(p,r)}:=\sum_{t\in E_{k}^{(p)}}\bar{\gamma}^{(p,r)}(t)\quad\text{where}\quad\bar{\gamma}^{(p,r)}(t):=\sup_{f\in\mathcal{P}_{k}^{(p)}}\mathbb{E}\left[|f(x_{t})-f^{\star}(x_{t})|^{r}\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{t}]\mid\mathcal{H}_{t-1}\right],\quad t\in E_{k}^{(p)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></munder><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext>where</mtext><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></munder><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>r</mi></msup><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\Gamma}_{k}^{(p,r)}:=\sum_{t\in E_{k}^{(p)}}\bar{\gamma}^{(p,r)}(t)\quad\text{where}\quad\bar{\gamma}^{(p,r)}(t):=\sup_{f\in\mathcal{P}_{k}^{(p)}}\mathbb{E}\left[|f(x_{t})-f^{\star}(x_{t})|^{r}\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{t}]\mid\mathcal{H}_{t-1}\right],\quad t\in E_{k}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p10.3" class="ltx_p">which intuitively quantifies the <math id="A3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> discrepancy between the queries on epoch <math id="A3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{k}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{k}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math> and queries prior to this epoch. Since within <math id="A3.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\Delta}_{k}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\Delta}_{k}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math>, we only focus on times when <math id="A3.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> coincide, the same proof as for <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Lemma 10]</cite> shows that with probability at least <math id="A3.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A3.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\Delta}_{k}^{(p)}\leq 5\bar{\Gamma}_{k}^{(p,2)}+16\ln\frac{T}{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>T</mi><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\Delta}_{k}^{(p)}\leq 5\bar{\Gamma}_{k}^{(p,2)}+16\ln\frac{T}{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A3.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in[P],k\in[N_{p}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in[P],k\in[N_{p}]</annotation></semantics></math>.
A main observation is that since all functions agree on <math id="A3.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\emptyset}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\emptyset}</annotation></semantics></math>, observing the value of <math id="A3.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> on this instance does not bring any new information. Formally, for all layers <math id="A3.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and epochs <math id="A3.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{k}^{(p)}\subseteq\tilde{\mathcal{P}}_{k}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>⊆</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{k}^{(p)}\subseteq\tilde{\mathcal{P}}_{k}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, for any <math id="A3.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in R_{k}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in R_{k}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math>, one has</p>
<table id="A3.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\gamma}^{(p,r)}(t)\leq\tilde{\gamma}^{(p,r)}(t):=\sup_{f\in\tilde{\mathcal{P}}_{k}^{(p)}}\mathbb{E}\left[|f(\tilde{x}_{t})-f^{\star}(\tilde{x}_{t})|^{r}\mid\mathcal{H}_{t-1}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\gamma}^{(p,r)}(t)\leq\tilde{\gamma}^{(p,r)}(t):=\sup_{f\in\tilde{\mathcal{P}}_{k}^{(p)}}\mathbb{E}\left[|f(\tilde{x}_{t})-f^{\star}(\tilde{x}_{t})|^{r}\mid\mathcal{H}_{t-1}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p10.4" class="ltx_p">In particular, we obtained <math id="A3.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\Gamma}_{k}^{(p,r)}\leq\tilde{\Gamma}_{k}^{(p,r)}:=\sum_{t\in E_{k}^{(p)}}\tilde{\gamma}^{(p,r)}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\Gamma}_{k}^{(p,r)}\leq\tilde{\Gamma}_{k}^{(p,r)}:=\sum_{t\in E_{k}^{(p)}}\tilde{\gamma}^{(p,r)}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A3.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, which corresponds to the quantity used within the original proof if we were working with the smoothed data <math id="A3.p10.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Altogether, the regret decomposition bound from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Equation (14)]</cite> shows that for any <math id="A3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}\in\{0,\ldots,P\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}\in\{0,\ldots,P\}</annotation></semantics></math>, with probability at least <math id="A3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-4\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-4\delta</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex39"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(\hat{y}_{t})-\ell_{t}(f^{\star}(x_{t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(\hat{y}_{t})-\ell_{t}(f^{\star}(x_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex40"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 12\sum_{p=\max(p_{0},1)}^{P}\sum_{k\in[N_{p}]}\sqrt{2\max\left(\Delta_{k}^{(p)},2\right)\ln\left(\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{F};\alpha,T)+1\right)}+8N_{p_{0}}\ln\frac{2T}{\delta}+\sum_{k\in[N_{p_{0}}]}\Lambda_{k}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>P</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 12\sum_{p=\max(p_{0},1)}^{P}\sum_{k\in[N_{p}]}\sqrt{2\max\left(\Delta_{k}^{(p)},2\right)\ln\left(\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{F};\alpha,T)+1\right)}+8N_{p_{0}}\ln\frac{2T}{\delta}+\sum_{k\in[N_{p_{0}}]}\Lambda_{k}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex41"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overset{(i)}{\leq}12\sum_{p=\max(p_{0},1)}^{P}\sum_{k\in[N_{p}]}\sqrt{2\max\left(\bar{\Delta}_{k}^{(p)},2\right)\ln\left(\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{F};\alpha,T)+1\right)}+8N_{p_{0}}\ln\frac{2T}{\delta}+\sum_{k\in[N_{p_{0}}]}\tilde{\Lambda}_{k}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>P</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overset{(i)}{\leq}12\sum_{p=\max(p_{0},1)}^{P}\sum_{k\in[N_{p}]}\sqrt{2\max\left(\bar{\Delta}_{k}^{(p)},2\right)\ln\left(\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{F};\alpha,T)+1\right)}+8N_{p_{0}}\ln\frac{2T}{\delta}+\sum_{k\in[N_{p_{0}}]}\tilde{\Lambda}_{k}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex42"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle+(12P\sqrt{2\ln\left(\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{F};\alpha,T)+1\right)}+1)\sum_{t\in[T]}\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>P</mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+(12P\sqrt{2\ln\left(\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{F};\alpha,T)+1\right)}+1)\sum_{t\in[T]}\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex43"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 12\sum_{p=\max(p_{0},1)}^{P}\sum_{k\in[N_{p}]}\sqrt{2\left(5\tilde{\Gamma}_{k}^{(p,2)}+16\ln\frac{T}{\delta}\right)\ln\left(\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{F};\alpha,T)+1\right)}+8N_{p_{0}}\ln\frac{2T}{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>P</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>T</mi><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 12\sum_{p=\max(p_{0},1)}^{P}\sum_{k\in[N_{p}]}\sqrt{2\left(5\tilde{\Gamma}_{k}^{(p,2)}+16\ln\frac{T}{\delta}\right)\ln\left(\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{F};\alpha,T)+1\right)}+8N_{p_{0}}\ln\frac{2T}{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex44"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle+\sum_{k\in[N_{p_{0}}]}\left(2\tilde{\Gamma}_{k}^{(p_{0},1)}+3\ln\frac{T}{\delta}\right)+(12P\sqrt{2\ln\left(\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{F};\alpha,T)+1\right)}+1)\sum_{t\in[T]}\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>ln</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>T</mi><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>P</mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\sum_{k\in[N_{p_{0}}]}\left(2\tilde{\Gamma}_{k}^{(p_{0},1)}+3\ln\frac{T}{\delta}\right)+(12P\sqrt{2\ln\left(\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{F};\alpha,T)+1\right)}+1)\sum_{t\in[T]}\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p11.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{F};\alpha,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{F};\alpha,T)</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="A3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-covering number of <math id="A3.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is the parameter used within the algorithm to construct coverings. In <math id="A3.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i)</annotation></semantics></math> we used <a href="#A3.E8" title="In Appendix C Proofs from ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eqs.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and <a href="#A3.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Appendix C Proofs from ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> together with the inequalities <math id="A3.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{a+b}\leq\sqrt{a}+\sqrt{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>a</mi></msqrt><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi>b</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{a+b}\leq\sqrt{a}+\sqrt{b}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A3.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{a}\leq a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>a</mi></msqrt><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{a}\leq a</annotation></semantics></math> for any non-negative integer.
Importantly, except for the term proportional to <math id="A3.p11.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sum_{t\in[T]}\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{t\in[T]}\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}]</annotation></semantics></math> the rest of the last term exactly corresponds to the regret bound from the original proof for the smoothed data <math id="A3.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, the rest of the proof directly applies. Also, in the binary classification setting, we can take the parameter <math id="A3.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math> and by Sauer-Shelah lemma <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> we have <math id="A3.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ln\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{F};\alpha,T)\lesssim d\ln T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ln\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{F};\alpha,T)\lesssim d\ln T</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, in the binary classification setting, the rest of the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Proposition 13]</cite> implies that for any <math id="A3.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\star}\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\star}\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, with probability at least <math id="A3.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex45"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(\hat{y}_{t})-\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(f^{\star}(x_{t}))\leq C\sqrt{\frac{(d\ln^{2}T+d\ln\ln\frac{1}{\delta}+\ln\frac{1}{\delta})\ln^{3}T}{\sigma}\cdot T}+C\sqrt{d\ln^{3}T}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ln</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ln</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>σ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ln</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(\hat{y}_{t})-\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(f^{\star}(x_{t}))\leq C\sqrt{\frac{(d\ln^{2}T+d\ln\ln\frac{1}{\delta}+\ln\frac{1}{\delta})\ln^{3}T}{\sigma}\cdot T}+C\sqrt{d\ln^{3}T}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p11.3" class="ltx_p">for some universal constant <math id="A3.p11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Further, from the last claim of <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ Coupling reduction lemma. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we can apply Freedman’s inequality <a href="#Thmtheorem13" title="Theorem 13 (Freedman’s inequality). ‣ B.2 Concentration inequlities ‣ Appendix B Further preliminaries ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> to the martingale increments <math id="A3.p11.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}]-\mathbb{P}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}\mid\tilde{x}_{&lt;t},x_{&lt;t}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}]-\mathbb{P}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}\mid\tilde{x}_{&lt;t},x_{&lt;t}]</annotation></semantics></math> to show that with probability at least <math id="A3.p11.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}]\leq 2\rho(\epsilon)T+2\ln\frac{1}{\delta}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>ln</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>δ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}]\leq 2\rho(\epsilon)T+2\ln\frac{1}{\delta}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">The last step of the proof is to generalize this result for oblivious benchmark <math id="A3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> to adaptive benchmarks (where <math id="A3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> may be data-dependent). The same arguments from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Section 5.2]</cite> apply here with minor adjustments. As in the original proof, we construct an <math id="A3.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-cover <math id="A3.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A3.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> for the base measure <math id="A3.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mu}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mu}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A3.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ln|\mathcal{H}|\leq 2d\ln(e^{2}/\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ln|\mathcal{H}|\leq 2d\ln(e^{2}/\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A3.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\frac{\sigma}{T}\ln\frac{T}{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>σ</mi><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>T</mi><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\frac{\sigma}{T}\ln\frac{T}{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> and bound the adaptive regret using the regret of the algorithm compared to functions within <math id="A3.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Eq (30)]</cite>):</p>
<table id="A3.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(\hat{y}_{t})-\inf_{f\in\mathcal{F}}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(f(x_{t}))\leq\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(\hat{y}_{t})-\inf_{f\in\mathcal{H}}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(f(x_{t}))+\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}}\sum_{t=1}^{T}g(x_{t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(\hat{y}_{t})-\inf_{f\in\mathcal{F}}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(f(x_{t}))\leq\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(\hat{y}_{t})-\inf_{f\in\mathcal{H}}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(f(x_{t}))+\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}}\sum_{t=1}^{T}g(x_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p12.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.p12.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{G}:=\{\mathbbm{1}[f\neq h_{f}],f\in\mathcal{F}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}:=\{\mathbbm{1}[f\neq h_{f}],f\in\mathcal{F}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The regret compared to <math id="A3.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded using the previous fixed-benchmark-high-probability regret bound and the union bound, as in the original proof. We then note that</p>
<table id="A3.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}}\sum_{t=1}^{T}g(x_{t})\leq\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}]+\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}}\sum_{t=1}^{T}g(\tilde{x}_{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></munder><munderover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></munder><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}}\sum_{t=1}^{T}g(x_{t})\leq\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}]+\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}}\sum_{t=1}^{T}g(\tilde{x}_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p12.3" class="ltx_p">Hence, from there we can use the same arguments as in the original proof to bound the last term. Putting everything together as in the original proof, together with <a href="#A3.E10" title="In Appendix C Proofs from ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, we obtained with probability at least <math id="A3.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-3\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-3\delta</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A3.Ex48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(\hat{y}_{t})-\inf_{f\in\mathcal{F}}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(f(x_{t}))\lesssim\sqrt{\frac{(d\ln^{2}T+d\ln\ln\frac{1}{\delta}+\ln\frac{1}{\delta})\ln^{3}T}{\sigma}\cdot T}+\rho(\epsilon)T\sqrt{d\ln^{3}T}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ln</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ln</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>σ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ln</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(\hat{y}_{t})-\inf_{f\in\mathcal{F}}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\ell_{t}(f(x_{t}))\lesssim\sqrt{\frac{(d\ln^{2}T+d\ln\ln\frac{1}{\delta}+\ln\frac{1}{\delta})\ln^{3}T}{\sigma}\cdot T}+\rho(\epsilon)T\sqrt{d\ln^{3}T}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p12.4" class="ltx_p">This ends the proof.
 <math id="A3.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\blacksquare" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\blacksquare</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Proofs from <a href="#S5" title="5 Distribution-class-dependent regret bounds ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a></h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first prove <a href="#Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 9. ‣ 5 Distribution-class-dependent regret bounds ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> which bounds the modulus of continuity of the envelope functional <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> has finite fragmentation numbers. This gives a quantitative version of <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2. ‣ 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof  of <a href="#Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 9. ‣ 5 Distribution-class-dependent regret bounds ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a></span>
The proof follows the same iterative construction as <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2. ‣ 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, but with parameters tuned to track the dependence on <math id="A4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix <math id="A4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the assumptions. We iteratively construct a sequence of distributions <math id="A4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu_{i})_{i\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu_{i})_{i\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> together with measurable sets <math id="A4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A_{i})_{i\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A_{i})_{i\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows. Suppose we have constructed <math id="A4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{j},A_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{j},A_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&lt;i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&lt;i</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="A4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. If there exists a measurable set <math id="A4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="A4.Ex49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j&lt;i}\mu_{j}(A)\leq\frac{\epsilon}{N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)}\quad\text{and}\quad\exists\mu\in\mathcal{U}\text{ with }\mu(A)\geq 2\epsilon," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> with </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j&lt;i}\mu_{j}(A)\leq\frac{\epsilon}{N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)}\quad\text{and}\quad\exists\mu\in\mathcal{U}\text{ with }\mu(A)\geq 2\epsilon,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">we fix <math id="A4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying this property and <math id="A4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}(A_{i})\geq 2\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}(A_{i})\geq 2\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Otherwise, we end the recursive construction. Next, if <math id="A4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined, we introduce <math id="A4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}:=A_{i}\setminus\bigcup_{j&gt;i}A_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">∖</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}:=A_{i}\setminus\bigcup_{j&gt;i}A_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. By construction, all sets <math id="A4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are disjoint and if <math id="A4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leq N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leq N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)+1</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A4.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}(B_{i})\geq\mu_{i}(A_{i})-\sum_{j&gt;i}\mu_{i}(A_{j})\geq 2\epsilon-(i-1)\frac{\epsilon}{N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)}\geq\epsilon." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}(B_{i})\geq\mu_{i}(A_{i})-\sum_{j&gt;i}\mu_{i}(A_{j})\geq 2\epsilon-(i-1)\frac{\epsilon}{N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)}\geq\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">By definition of <math id="A4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>, this implies that the construction must have ended at some index <math id="A4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{\max}\leq N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{\max}\leq N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>. We then pose <math id="A4.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\epsilon}:=\frac{1}{i_{\max}}\sum_{j=1}^{i_{\max}}\mu_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></msubsup><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\epsilon}:=\frac{1}{i_{\max}}\sum_{j=1}^{i_{\max}}\mu_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, for any measurable set <math id="A4.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subseteq\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subseteq\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A4.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\epsilon}(A)\leq\frac{\epsilon}{N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\epsilon}(A)\leq\frac{\epsilon}{N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> we have</p>
<table id="A4.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j=1}^{i_{\max}}\mu_{j}(A)\leq i_{\max}\frac{\epsilon}{N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)^{2}}\leq\frac{\epsilon}{N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j=1}^{i_{\max}}\mu_{j}(A)\leq i_{\max}\frac{\epsilon}{N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)^{2}}\leq\frac{\epsilon}{N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Since the construction ended at <math id="A4.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>, this precisely implies that <math id="A4.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A)\leq 2\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A)\leq 2\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, ending the proof.
 <math id="A4.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\blacksquare" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\blacksquare</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We next prove the regret lower bound from <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 8. ‣ 5 Distribution-class-dependent regret bounds ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> based on fragmentation numbers.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof  of <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 8. ‣ 5 Distribution-class-dependent regret bounds ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a></span>
The proof uses classical arguments from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> which gives a lower bound <math id="A4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(\sqrt{\text{LD}(\mathcal{F})T}\wedge T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mtext>LD</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>∧</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(\sqrt{\text{LD}(\mathcal{F})T}\wedge T)</annotation></semantics></math> on the regret for adversarial online learning for a function class <math id="A4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We recall the definition of the Littlestone dimension <math id="A4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{LD}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>LD</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{LD}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#Thmdefinition10" title="Definition 10 (Littlestone Dimension). ‣ B.1 Additional definitions ‣ Appendix B Further preliminaries ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> in the appendix for completeness.
Fix <math id="A4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and for convenience, denote <math id="A4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1},\ldots,A_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1},\ldots,A_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> be disjoint measurable sets such that for all <math id="A4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[N]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A_{i})\geq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A_{i})\geq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{j}:=\{i\in[N]:i\in[(j-1)N/d,jN/d]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{j}:=\{i\in[N]:i\in[(j-1)N/d,jN/d]\}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[d]</annotation></semantics></math>. For convenience, we enumerate <math id="A4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{j}:=\{i_{l}^{(j)},l\in[N_{j}]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{j}:=\{i_{l}^{(j)},l\in[N_{j}]\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="A4.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in\{0,\ldots,N_{1}\}\times\ldots\times\{0,\ldots,N_{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in\{0,\ldots,N_{1}\}\times\ldots\times\{0,\ldots,N_{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the function <math id="A4.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{l}:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{l}:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> via</p>
<table id="A4.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{l}(x):=\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{if }x\in\bigcup_{l&gt;l_{j}}A_{i_{l}^{(j)}},\;j\in[d]\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mstyle><msub><mi>A</mi><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{l}(x):=\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{if }x\in\bigcup_{l&gt;l_{j}}A_{i_{l}^{(j)}},\;j\in[d]\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">We then consider the function class <math id="A4.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> which contains all such functions. For convenience, we also consider a simplified version of this function class <math id="A4.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}^{\prime}:I_{1}\sqcup\ldots\sqcup I_{d}\to\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊔</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>⊔</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}^{\prime}:I_{1}\sqcup\ldots\sqcup I_{d}\to\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> where we identify all sets <math id="A4.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to a single point <math id="A4.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>: it is the product of thresholds on each set <math id="A4.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A4.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[d]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The Littlestone dimension of both function classes is the sum of the Littlestone dimension of each underlying threshold function class:</p>
<table id="A4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{LD}(\mathcal{F})=\text{LD}(\mathcal{F}^{\prime})\geq\sum_{j\in[d]}\left\lceil\log_{2}(|I_{j}|+1)\right\rceil\gtrsim(d\wedge N)\log_{2}(\left\lceil N/d\right\rceil+1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>LD</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>LD</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≥</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≳</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>⌉</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{LD}(\mathcal{F})=\text{LD}(\mathcal{F}^{\prime})\geq\sum_{j\in[d]}\left\lceil\log_{2}(|I_{j}|+1)\right\rceil\gtrsim(d\wedge N)\log_{2}(\left\lceil N/d\right\rceil+1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Fix a learning algorithm <math id="A4.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="alg" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">alg</annotation></semantics></math>. We then emulate the proof of the oblivious regret lower bound for online learning on this function class for a horizon <math id="A4.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}:=\left\lceil\epsilon T/2\right\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}:=\left\lceil\epsilon T/2\right\rceil</annotation></semantics></math> as follows. For each new iteration <math id="A4.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A4.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{t}\in I_{1}\sqcup\ldots\sqcup I_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊔</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>⊔</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{t}\in I_{1}\sqcup\ldots\sqcup I_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be the next element which would be chosen by the online learning lower bound adversary. At time <math id="A4.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, we then define <math id="A4.p4.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to be a distribution such that <math id="A4.p4.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}(A_{i_{t}})\geq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}(A_{i_{t}})\geq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, which exists by construction. If the sample <math id="A4.p4.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}\sim\mu_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}\sim\mu_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="A4.p4.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}\in A_{i_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}\in A_{i_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use the same labeling <math id="A4.p4.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as the online learning lower bound adversary. Otherwise, we randomly label <math id="A4.p4.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}\sim\mathcal{B}(1/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}\sim\mathcal{B}(1/2)</annotation></semantics></math> and ignore this iteration for the online learning adversary. Conditionally on the event <math id="A4.p4.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> that we were able to emulate <math id="A4.p4.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> iterations of the online learning adversary, the regret lower bound for online learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> implies the desired lower bound</p>
<table id="A4.Ex53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Reg}_{T}(\text{alg};\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U}\mid\mathcal{E})\gtrsim\sqrt{\textrm{LD}(\mathcal{F})T^{\prime}}\wedge T^{\prime}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>Reg</mtext><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>alg</mtext><mo>;</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≳</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mtext>LD</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>∧</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Reg}_{T}(\text{alg};\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U}\mid\mathcal{E})\gtrsim\sqrt{\textrm{LD}(\mathcal{F})T^{\prime}}\wedge T^{\prime},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p4.4" class="ltx_p">since the ignored iterations can only increase the regret in expectation. Here, <math id="A4.p4.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Reg}_{T}(\text{alg};\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U}\mid\mathcal{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>Reg</mtext><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>alg</mtext><mo>;</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Reg}_{T}(\text{alg};\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U}\mid\mathcal{E})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the conditional regret on <math id="A4.p4.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>. By construction, each iteration <math id="A4.p4.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math> emulates a step of the online learning adversary with probability <math id="A4.p4.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, by Azuma-Hoeffding’s inequality,</p>
<table id="A4.Ex54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}[\mathcal{E}^{c}]=\mathbb{P}\left[\sum_{t\in[T]}\mathbbm{1}[x_{t}\in A_{i_{t}}]&lt;\frac{\epsilon T}{2}\right]\overset{(i)}{\leq}\mathbb{P}_{Y\sim\mathcal{B}(T,\epsilon)}\left[Y&lt;\frac{\epsilon T}{2}\right]\overset{(ii)}{\leq}e^{-\epsilon T/8}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mover><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mover><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}[\mathcal{E}^{c}]=\mathbb{P}\left[\sum_{t\in[T]}\mathbbm{1}[x_{t}\in A_{i_{t}}]&lt;\frac{\epsilon T}{2}\right]\overset{(i)}{\leq}\mathbb{P}_{Y\sim\mathcal{B}(T,\epsilon)}\left[Y&lt;\frac{\epsilon T}{2}\right]\overset{(ii)}{\leq}e^{-\epsilon T/8}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p4.5" class="ltx_p">In <math id="A4.p4.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i)</annotation></semantics></math> we stochastically dominated the quantity of interest by a binomial of parameters <math id="A4.p4.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.p4.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, and in <math id="A4.p4.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ii)</annotation></semantics></math> we used Chernoff’s inequality. Therefore, the final regret lower bound becomes</p>
<table id="A4.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.Ex55"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Reg}_{T}(\text{alg};\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>Reg</mtext><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>alg</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Reg}_{T}(\text{alg};\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex55.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\text{Reg}_{T}(\text{alg};\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U}\mid\mathcal{E})(1-\mathbb{P}[\mathcal{E}^{c}])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>Reg</mtext><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>alg</mtext><mo>;</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\text{Reg}_{T}(\text{alg};\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U}\mid\mathcal{E})(1-\mathbb{P}[\mathcal{E}^{c}])</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.Ex56"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\gtrsim\sqrt{\text{LD}(\mathcal{F})\epsilon T}\wedge(\epsilon T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≳</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mtext>LD</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\gtrsim\sqrt{\text{LD}(\mathcal{F})\epsilon T}\wedge(\epsilon T)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.Ex57"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\gtrsim\sqrt{\epsilon(d\wedge N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon))T\ln\left(\frac{N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)}{d}+e\right)}\wedge(\epsilon T)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≳</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∧</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\gtrsim\sqrt{\epsilon(d\wedge N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon))T\ln\left(\frac{N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)}{d}+e\right)}\wedge(\epsilon T),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p4.6" class="ltx_p">where in the last inequality we used <a href="#A4.E11" title="In Appendix D Proofs from ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>. This implies the desired result when <math id="A4.p4.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3d\leq N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3d\leq N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>.
 <math id="A4.p4.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\blacksquare" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\blacksquare</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we prove the regret upper bound from <a href="#Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 10. ‣ 5 Distribution-class-dependent regret bounds ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> for <math id="A4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>-dependent algorithms. This uses a quantitative variant of the coupling <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ Coupling reduction lemma. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> whose proof is deferred to <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E A quantitative coupling lemma ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof  of <a href="#Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 10. ‣ 5 Distribution-class-dependent regret bounds ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a></span>
Fix a distribution class <math id="A4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and a function class <math id="A4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> of VC dimension <math id="A4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.
Fix <math id="A4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. From <a href="#Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 9. ‣ 5 Distribution-class-dependent regret bounds ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, we can construct a distribution <math id="A4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any measurable set <math id="A4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subseteq\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subseteq\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\epsilon}(A)\leq\frac{\epsilon}{N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\epsilon}(A)\leq\frac{\epsilon}{N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, one has <math id="A4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A)\leq 2\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A)\leq 2\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Using <a href="#Thmtheorem15" title="Lemma 15. ‣ Appendix E A quantitative coupling lemma ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>, we then couple the original adversary with a sequence <math id="A4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{x}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{x}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by an <math id="A4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon/(4N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)^{2}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon/(4N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)^{2}))</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth adversary (with respect to a base measure <math id="A4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mu}_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mu}_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>) such that for all <math id="A4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A4.Ex58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}[\tilde{x}_{t}\neq x_{t}\mid\tilde{x}_{&lt;t},x_{&lt;t}]\leq 2\epsilon." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}[\tilde{x}_{t}\neq x_{t}\mid\tilde{x}_{&lt;t},x_{&lt;t}]\leq 2\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p7.2" class="ltx_p">This reduces the analysis to the smoothed model, plus an additive term accounting for coupling failures.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, consider the algorithm <math id="A4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="alg_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">alg_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> that runs Hedge over an <math id="A4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-cover of <math id="A4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the base measure <math id="A4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mu}_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mu}_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>; denote such a cover by <math id="A4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="A4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a tuning parameter. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Theorem 3.1]</cite>, we can bound the expected regret of <math id="A4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="alg_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">alg_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="A4.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.Ex59"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{O}\left(\sqrt{T\ln|\mathcal{H}(\alpha)|}\right)+\mathbb{E}\left[\max_{f\in\mathcal{F}}\min_{g\in\mathcal{F}(\alpha)}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}[f(x_{t})\neq g(x_{t})]\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></munder><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{O}\left(\sqrt{T\ln|\mathcal{H}(\alpha)|}\right)+\mathbb{E}\left[\max_{f\in\mathcal{F}}\min_{g\in\mathcal{F}(\alpha)}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}[f(x_{t})\neq g(x_{t})]\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.Ex60"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\mathcal{O}\left(\sqrt{T\ln|\mathcal{H}(\alpha)|}\right)+\mathbb{E}\left[\max_{f\in\mathcal{F}}\min_{g\in\mathcal{F}(\alpha)}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}[f(\tilde{x}_{t})\neq g(\tilde{x}_{t})]\right]+\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}\neq x_{t}]\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></munder><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\mathcal{O}\left(\sqrt{T\ln|\mathcal{H}(\alpha)|}\right)+\mathbb{E}\left[\max_{f\in\mathcal{F}}\min_{g\in\mathcal{F}(\alpha)}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}[f(\tilde{x}_{t})\neq g(\tilde{x}_{t})]\right]+\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbbm{1}[\tilde{x}_{t}\neq x_{t}]\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p8.2" class="ltx_p">By construction, the last term is bounded by <math id="A4.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\epsilon T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\epsilon T</annotation></semantics></math>. The first two terms exactly correspond to the regret analysis for the smoothed data <math id="A4.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{\leq T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{\leq T}</annotation></semantics></math>. In turn, the arguments from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Theorem 3.1]</cite> directly imply that with an appropriate value of <math id="A4.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A4.Ex61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Reg}_{T}(alg_{\alpha};\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U})\lesssim\sqrt{Td\ln\left(\frac{4TN_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)^{2}}{d\epsilon}\right)}+d\ln\left(\frac{4TN_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)^{2}}{d\epsilon}\right)+\epsilon T." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>Reg</mtext><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Reg}_{T}(alg_{\alpha};\mathcal{F},\mathcal{U})\lesssim\sqrt{Td\ln\left(\frac{4TN_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)^{2}}{d\epsilon}\right)}+d\ln\left(\frac{4TN_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)^{2}}{d\epsilon}\right)+\epsilon T.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p8.3" class="ltx_p">We then minimize this value over <math id="A4.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. First, note that without loss of generality we may focus on <math id="A4.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\geq 1/T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\geq 1/T</annotation></semantics></math>: smaller values cannot improve the right-hand side by more than a constant factor, since the first term is already <math id="A4.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(1)</annotation></semantics></math> in that regime.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we can ignore the second term on the right-hand side: if it dominates the first term, then necessarily <math id="A4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\ln\left(\frac{4TN_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)^{2}}{d\epsilon}\right)\gtrsim T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≳</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\ln\left(\frac{4TN_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)^{2}}{d\epsilon}\right)\gtrsim T</annotation></semantics></math>, in which case the bound is already trivial (it exceeds <math id="A4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>).
Tuning the parameter <math id="A4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in(1/T,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in(1/T,1]</annotation></semantics></math> then gives the desired result.
 <math id="A4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\blacksquare" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\blacksquare</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>A quantitative coupling lemma</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The next lemma is a variant of <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ Coupling reduction lemma. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> that is convenient when one only has a guarantee of the form
<math id="A5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}(A)\leq\epsilon\Rightarrow\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A)\leq\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}(A)\leq\epsilon\Rightarrow\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A)\leq\eta</annotation></semantics></math> for some base measure <math id="A5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 15</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> be a generalized smoothed distribution class on <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon,\eta\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon,\eta\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be a probability measure such that for any measurable set <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subseteq\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subseteq\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}(A)\leq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}(A)\leq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A)\leq\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A)\leq\eta</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider any <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>-constrained adaptive adversary generating samples <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix a dummy symbol <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\emptyset}\notin\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\emptyset}\notin\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem15.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem15.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem15.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then there exists a coupling with a sequence <math id="Thmtheorem15.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{x}_{T}\in\mathcal{X}\cup\{x_{\emptyset}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{x}_{T}\in\mathcal{X}\cup\{x_{\emptyset}\}</annotation></semantics></math> which is generated by an <math id="Thmtheorem15.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon/4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon/4)</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth adversary on <math id="Thmtheorem15.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\cup\{x_{\emptyset}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\cup\{x_{\emptyset}\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="Thmtheorem15.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<ol id="A5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{t}\in\{x_{t},x_{\emptyset}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{t}\in\{x_{t},x_{\emptyset}\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}\mid\tilde{x}_{&lt;t},x_{&lt;t}]\leq\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}\mid\tilde{x}_{&lt;t},x_{&lt;t}]\leq\eta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof  </span>The proof is similar to that of <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ Coupling reduction lemma. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.
Denote by <math id="A5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> the distribution of <math id="A5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> conditional on the past history <math id="A5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{&lt;t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{&lt;t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Define the mixture base measure</p>
<table id="A5.Ex62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mu}_{0}:=\tfrac{1}{2}\mu_{0}+\tfrac{1}{2}\delta_{x_{\emptyset}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mu}_{0}:=\tfrac{1}{2}\mu_{0}+\tfrac{1}{2}\delta_{x_{\emptyset}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">For convenience, set <math id="A5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:=\eta/\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:=\eta/\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="A5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math>, construct <math id="A5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="A5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="A5.Ex63" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{t}:=\begin{cases}x_{t}&amp;\text{if }\frac{d\mu_{t}}{d\mu_{0}}(x_{t})\leq 2\sigma\text{ or }\mu_{0}(\{x_{t}\})&gt;\epsilon,\\
x_{\emptyset}&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> or </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{t}:=\begin{cases}x_{t}&amp;\text{if }\frac{d\mu_{t}}{d\mu_{0}}(x_{t})\leq 2\sigma\text{ or }\mu_{0}(\{x_{t}\})&gt;\epsilon,\\
x_{\emptyset}&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mu}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mu}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> be the distribution of <math id="A5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> conditional on <math id="A5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{&lt;t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{&lt;t}</annotation></semantics></math>. As in the proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ Coupling reduction lemma. ‣ 4 Universal Algorithms ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, one checks that the density ratio is bounded by <math id="A5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4/\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4/\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="A5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mu}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mu}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="A5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{x}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{x}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon/4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon/4)</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Next, for any <math id="A5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math>, define</p>
<table id="A5.Ex64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{t}:=\Bigl\{x\in\mathcal{X}:\tfrac{d\mu_{t}}{d\mu_{0}}(x)&gt;2\sigma\Bigr\}\cap\Bigl\{x\in\mathcal{X}:\mu_{0}(\{x\})\leq\epsilon\Bigr\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{t}:=\Bigl\{x\in\mathcal{X}:\tfrac{d\mu_{t}}{d\mu_{0}}(x)&gt;2\sigma\Bigr\}\cap\Bigl\{x\in\mathcal{X}:\mu_{0}(\{x\})\leq\epsilon\Bigr\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p4.2" class="ltx_p">By construction,</p>
<table id="A5.Ex65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}\mid\tilde{x}_{&lt;t},x_{&lt;t}]=\mu_{t}(A_{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}[\tilde{x}_{t}=x_{\emptyset}\mid\tilde{x}_{&lt;t},x_{&lt;t}]=\mu_{t}(A_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}(A_{t})\leq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}(A_{t})\leq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, then by assumption <math id="A5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}(A_{t})\leq\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A_{t})\leq\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}(A_{t})\leq\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(A_{t})\leq\eta</annotation></semantics></math>.
Otherwise, suppose <math id="A5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}(A_{t})&gt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}(A_{t})&gt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. By <a href="#Thmtheorem16" title="Lemma 16. ‣ Appendix E A quantitative coupling lemma ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>, there exists a measurable <math id="A5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\subseteq A_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\subseteq A_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}(B)\in(\epsilon/2,\epsilon]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}(B)\in(\epsilon/2,\epsilon]</annotation></semantics></math>. On the one hand, by the defining property of <math id="A5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="A5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}(B)\geq 2\sigma\,\mu_{0}(B)&gt;\sigma\epsilon=\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}(B)\geq 2\sigma\,\mu_{0}(B)&gt;\sigma\epsilon=\eta</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, <math id="A5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}(B)\leq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}(B)\leq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="A5.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}(B)\leq\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(B)\leq\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}(B)\leq\mu_{\mathcal{U}}(B)\leq\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction.
Therefore <math id="A5.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}(A_{t})\leq\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}(A_{t})\leq\eta</annotation></semantics></math> in all cases, completing the proof.
 <math id="A5.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\blacksquare" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\blacksquare</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 16</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> be a probability measure on <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subseteq\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subseteq\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be measurable such that <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(A)&gt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(A)&gt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and for all <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in A</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\{x\})\leq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\{x\})\leq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists a measurable set <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\subseteq A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\subseteq A</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(B)\in(\epsilon/2,\epsilon]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(B)\in(\epsilon/2,\epsilon]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof  </span>If there exists an atom <math id="A5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in A</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\{x\})\in(\epsilon/2,\epsilon]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\{x\})\in(\epsilon/2,\epsilon]</annotation></semantics></math>, then take <math id="A5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\{x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\{x\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Otherwise, every atom in <math id="A5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> has mass at most <math id="A5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\mathrm{at}}\subseteq A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>at</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\mathrm{at}}\subseteq A</annotation></semantics></math> be the (at most countable) set of atoms and write <math id="A5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m:=\mu(A_{\mathrm{at}})\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>at</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m:=\mu(A_{\mathrm{at}})\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="A5.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math>, pick a finite subset of atoms <math id="A5.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\}\subseteq A_{\mathrm{at}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>at</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\}\subseteq A_{\mathrm{at}}</annotation></semantics></math> by adding atoms greedily until the partial sum first exceeds <math id="A5.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math>. Since each atom has mass at most <math id="A5.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math>, the resulting set <math id="A5.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="A5.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(B)\in(\epsilon/2,\epsilon]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(B)\in(\epsilon/2,\epsilon]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&lt;\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&lt;\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math>, then the non-atomic part <math id="A5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\setminus A_{\mathrm{at}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>at</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\setminus A_{\mathrm{at}}</annotation></semantics></math> has measure <math id="A5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(A\setminus A_{\mathrm{at}})&gt;\epsilon-m&gt;\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>at</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(A\setminus A_{\mathrm{at}})&gt;\epsilon-m&gt;\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A5.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> restricted to <math id="A5.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\setminus A_{\mathrm{at}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>at</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\setminus A_{\mathrm{at}}</annotation></semantics></math> is non-atomic, there exists a measurable <math id="A5.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subseteq A\setminus A_{\mathrm{at}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>at</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subseteq A\setminus A_{\mathrm{at}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A5.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(C)=\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(C)=\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math>. Taking <math id="A5.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B:=A_{\mathrm{at}}\cup C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>at</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B:=A_{\mathrm{at}}\cup C</annotation></semantics></math> yields <math id="A5.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(B)=m+\epsilon/2\in(\epsilon/2,\epsilon]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(B)=m+\epsilon/2\in(\epsilon/2,\epsilon]</annotation></semantics></math>.
 <math id="A5.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\blacksquare" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\blacksquare</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Proofs from <a href="#S6" title="6 Differential Privacy ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a></h2>

<section id="A6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.1 </span>Proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem11" title="Theorem 11. ‣ 6 Differential Privacy ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a></h3>

<div id="A6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We start by showing the following lemma which is a consequence of Turán’s theorem which lower bounds the independence number of graphs with few edges.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 17</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem17.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon,\delta\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon,\delta\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> be a distribution class such that <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)\geq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)\geq N</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, there exist <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq N\delta/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq N\delta/2</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint measurable sets <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1},\ldots,A_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1},\ldots,A_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> together with distributions <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1},\ldots,\mu_{k}\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1},\ldots,\mu_{k}\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<ol id="A6.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all </span><math id="A6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[k]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A6.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="A6.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}(A_{i})\geq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}(A_{i})\geq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A6.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and for all </span><math id="A6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j\in[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j\in[k]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A6.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="A6.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}(A_{j})\leq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}(A_{j})\leq\delta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A6.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="Thmtheorem17.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem17.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Additionally, if <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, then there exist disjoint measurable sets <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A_{i})_{i\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A_{i})_{i\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>, distributions <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu_{i})_{i\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu_{i})_{i\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}(A_{i})\geq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}(A_{i})\geq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, and for all <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A6.Ex66" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{2^{n}\leq i\neq j&lt;2^{n+1}}\mu_{i}(A_{j})\leq 2^{-n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{2^{n}\leq i\neq j&lt;2^{n+1}}\mu_{i}(A_{j})\leq 2^{-n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof  </span>Fix <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)\geq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)\geq N</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exist <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1},\ldots,B_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1},\ldots,B_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint measurable sets together with distributions <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{1},\ldots,\nu_{N}\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{1},\ldots,\nu_{N}\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{i}(B_{i})\geq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{i}(B_{i})\geq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[N]</annotation></semantics></math>. Construct the graph <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}=([N],E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}=([N],E)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[N]</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)\in E</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{i}(B_{j})\geq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{i}(B_{j})\geq\delta</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{j}(B_{i})\geq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{j}(B_{i})\geq\delta</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> has at most <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N/\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N/\delta</annotation></semantics></math> edges since for each <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[N]</annotation></semantics></math> there can be at most <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\delta</annotation></semantics></math> indices <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[N]</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{i}(B_{j})\geq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{i}(B_{j})\geq\delta</annotation></semantics></math> given that all sets <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are disjoint. Therefore, <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> has average node degree at most <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, Turán’s theorem shows that there is an independent set <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of size at least <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N/(1+1/\delta)\geq N\delta/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N/(1+1/\delta)\geq N\delta/2</annotation></semantics></math>. Focusing only on the indices <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in S</annotation></semantics></math> with the corresponding sets <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and distribution <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> gives the first desired result.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We now prove the second claim when <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix a sequence <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B_{j})_{j\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B_{j})_{j\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> of disjoint measurable sets and corresponding distributions <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\nu_{j})_{j\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\nu_{j})_{j\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{j}(B_{j})\geq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{j}(B_{j})\geq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. We then essentially stitch together the above construction for the parameters <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{n}=2^{-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{n}=2^{-n}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, having constructed indices <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{i}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{i}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;2^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;2^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we focus on indices that have not been used yet <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{n}:=\mathbb{N}\setminus\{j_{l}:l&lt;2^{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{n}:=\mathbb{N}\setminus\{j_{l}:l&lt;2^{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Among the first <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> elements of <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the previous arguments show that we can construct distinct indices <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{j}\in\mathcal{I}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{j}\in\mathcal{I}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[2^{n},2^{n+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[2^{n},2^{n+1})</annotation></semantics></math> such that letting <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{j}=B_{i_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{j}=B_{i_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{j}=\nu_{i_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{j}=\nu_{i_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{i}(A_{j})\leq 2^{-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{i}(A_{j})\leq 2^{-n}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j\in[2^{n},2^{n+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j\in[2^{n},2^{n+1})</annotation></semantics></math>. This proves the desired properties and ends the proof.
 <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\blacksquare" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\blacksquare</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">With this tool at hand we now show that algorithms that privately learn generalized thresholds under <math id="A6.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> distributions at scale <math id="A6.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> with sample complexity <math id="A6.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, induce private algorithms for learning thresholds of size <math id="A6.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(\sqrt{N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)/m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(\sqrt{N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)/m})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 18</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem18.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem18.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> be a convex distribution class. Fix <math id="Thmtheorem18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem18.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. If for any generalized threshold function class <math id="Thmtheorem18.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an <math id="Thmtheorem18.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-differentially private <math id="Thmtheorem18.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>-accurate algorithm on distributions in <math id="Thmtheorem18.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> with sample complexity <math id="Thmtheorem18.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, then there exists an <math id="Thmtheorem18.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-differentially private <math id="Thmtheorem18.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2\alpha/\eta,2\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2\alpha/\eta,2\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>-accurate PAC learner for thresholds in <math id="Thmtheorem18.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[K]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[K]</annotation></semantics></math> with sample complexity <math id="Thmtheorem18.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmtheorem18.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\Omega\left(\sqrt{\frac{\beta N_{\mathcal{U}}(\eta)}{m}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\Omega\left(\sqrt{\frac{\beta N_{\mathcal{U}}(\eta)}{m}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem18.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem18.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem18.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Next, suppose that <math id="Thmtheorem18.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathcal{U}}(\eta)=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathcal{U}}(\eta)=\infty</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="Thmtheorem18.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, if for any generalized threshold function class <math id="Thmtheorem18.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an <math id="Thmtheorem18.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-differentially private <math id="Thmtheorem18.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>-accurate algorithm on distributions in <math id="Thmtheorem18.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> with some sample complexity <math id="Thmtheorem18.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(\mathcal{F})&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(\mathcal{F})&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, then there exists <math id="Thmtheorem18.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and an <math id="Thmtheorem18.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-differentially private <math id="Thmtheorem18.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2\alpha/\eta,2\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2\alpha/\eta,2\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>-accurate PAC learner for thresholds in <math id="Thmtheorem18.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[K]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[K]</annotation></semantics></math> with sample complexity <math id="Thmtheorem18.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="Thmtheorem18.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof  </span>Let <math id="A6.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> be a parameter to be fixed later. We first use <a href="#Thmtheorem17" title="Lemma 17. ‣ F.1 Proof of ‣ Appendix F Proofs from ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a> to construct <math id="A6.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\left\lfloor N_{\mathcal{U}}(\eta)\gamma/2\right\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\left\lfloor N_{\mathcal{U}}(\eta)\gamma/2\right\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint measurable sets <math id="A6.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and distributions <math id="A6.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A6.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[K]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[K]</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="A6.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}(A_{i})\geq\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}(A_{i})\geq\eta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}(A_{j})\leq\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}(A_{j})\leq\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A6.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j\in[K]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j\in[K]</annotation></semantics></math>. We now consider the generalized threshold class on these sets <math id="A6.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{F}:=\{x\in\mathcal{X}\mapsto\mathbbm{1}[x\in\bigcup_{j\geq i}A_{j}]:i\in[K+1]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}:=\{x\in\mathcal{X}\mapsto\mathbbm{1}[x\in\bigcup_{j\geq i}A_{j}]:i\in[K+1]\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Given an <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-differentially private <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>-accurate algorithm for <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> on distributions in <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> with sample complexity <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct a new algorithm <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows. For any dataset <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\prime}=(a_{i},y_{i})_{i\in[m]}\in([K]\times\{0,1\})^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\prime}=(a_{i},y_{i})_{i\in[m]}\in([K]\times\{0,1\})^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, we replace each datapoint <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{i},y_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{i},y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(x_{i},y_{i}^{\prime}:=y_{i}\mathbbm{1}[x_{i}\in A_{i}])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{i},y_{i}^{\prime}:=y_{i}\mathbbm{1}[x_{i}\in A_{i}])</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\sim\mu_{a_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\sim\mu_{a_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> are sampled independently for each <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[m]</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote by <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> the corresponding dataset and let <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> be the output of <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> returns the vector <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m17" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\mathbbm{1}[\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mu_{i}}[h(x)\mid x\in A_{i}]\geq 1/2])_{i\in[K]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbbm{1}[\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mu_{i}}[h(x)\mid x\in A_{i}]\geq 1/2])_{i\in[K]}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-private, so is the algorithm that would directly output the hypothesis <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> (note that <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> can be viewed as a mixture of <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-private algorithms, one for each potential realization of <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\sim\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\sim\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[K]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[K]</annotation></semantics></math>). Hence <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is also <math id="A6.SS1.p6.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-private.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We next show that <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a PAC learner for the class of thresholds on <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[K]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[K]</annotation></semantics></math> which we denote by <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{F}^{\prime}:=\{i\in[K]\mapsto\mathbbm{1}[i\geq i_{0}]:i_{0}\in[K+1]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}^{\prime}:=\{i\in[K]\mapsto\mathbbm{1}[i\geq i_{0}]:i_{0}\in[K+1]\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix any distribution <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[K]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[K]</annotation></semantics></math> and a threshold <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\prime}=(a_{i},y_{i})_{i\in[m]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\prime}=(a_{i},y_{i})_{i\in[m]}</annotation></semantics></math> be a dataset corresponding of <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. samples from <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> labeled by <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that for any sample <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[m]</annotation></semantics></math>, letting <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\sim\mu_{a_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\sim\mu_{a_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the sampled instance, if <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\notin\bigcup_{j\neq i}A_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">∉</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\notin\bigcup_{j\neq i}A_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the value <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y_{i}\mathbbm{1}[x_{i}\in A_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}\mathbbm{1}[x_{i}\in A_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> is consistent with the function <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g:x\in\mathcal{X}\mapsto\mathbbm{1}[x\in\bigcup_{i\in[K]:g^{\prime}(i)=1}A_{i}]\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:x\in\mathcal{X}\mapsto\mathbbm{1}[x\in\bigcup_{i\in[K]:g^{\prime}(i)=1}A_{i}]\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, under the event</p>
<table id="A6.Ex67" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}:=\left\{\forall i\in[m]:x_{i}\notin\bigcup_{j\neq i}A_{j}\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">∉</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}:=\left\{\forall i\in[m]:x_{i}\notin\bigcup_{j\neq i}A_{j}\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS1.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="A6.SS1.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with a dataset composed of <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. samples from the mixture <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu:=\sum_{i\in[K]}\nu_{i}\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu:=\sum_{i\in[K]}\nu_{i}\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> labeled by <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is convex and that by construction,</p>
<table id="A6.Ex68" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}[\mathcal{E}]\leq mK\gamma." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}[\mathcal{E}]\leq mK\gamma.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS1.p7.3" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>-accurate, this shows that with probability at least <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\beta-mK\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\beta-mK\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> on the sampled dataset <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, the output <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> on the corresponding dataset <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</p>
<table id="A6.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A6.Ex69"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.Ex69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.Ex69.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\mathbb{P}_{x\sim\mu}[h(x)\neq g(x)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\mathbb{P}_{x\sim\mu}[h(x)\neq g(x)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A6.Ex70"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.Ex70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\mathbb{E}_{i\sim\nu}\left[\mu_{i}(A_{i})\mathbb{P}_{x\sim\mu_{i}}[h(x)\neq g^{\prime}(i)\mid x\in A_{i}]\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\mathbb{E}_{i\sim\nu}\left[\mu_{i}(A_{i})\mathbb{P}_{x\sim\mu_{i}}[h(x)\neq g^{\prime}(i)\mid x\in A_{i}]\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A6.Ex71"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.Ex71.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\frac{\eta}{2}\mathbb{E}_{i\sim\nu}[h^{\prime}(i)\neq g^{\prime}(i)]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≥</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\frac{\eta}{2}\mathbb{E}_{i\sim\nu}[h^{\prime}(i)\neq g^{\prime}(i)],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS1.p7.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the final output of <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. This shows that <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2\alpha/\eta,\beta+mK\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2\alpha/\eta,\beta+mK\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>-accurate PAC learner for <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="mK\gamma\leq mN_{\mathcal{U}}(\eta)\gamma^{2}/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">mK\gamma\leq mN_{\mathcal{U}}(\eta)\gamma^{2}/2</annotation></semantics></math>. Taking <math id="A6.SS1.p7.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\sqrt{\frac{2\beta}{mN_{\mathcal{U}}(\eta)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\sqrt{\frac{2\beta}{mN_{\mathcal{U}}(\eta)}}</annotation></semantics></math> gives the desired result for the first claim.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The second claim is proved analogously. Suppose that <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathcal{U}}(\eta)=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathcal{U}}(\eta)=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we use <a href="#Thmtheorem17" title="Lemma 17. ‣ F.1 Proof of ‣ Appendix F Proofs from ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a> to construct disjoint measurable sets <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and distributions <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}(A_{i})\geq\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}(A_{i})\geq\eta</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, and for all <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j\in[2^{n},2^{n+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j\in[2^{n},2^{n+1})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}(A_{j})\leq 2^{-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}(A_{j})\leq 2^{-n}</annotation></semantics></math>. As above, we construct the generalized threshold class on these sets <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{F}:=\{x\in\mathcal{X}\mapsto\mathbbm{1}[x\in\bigcup_{j\geq i}A_{j}]:i\geq 1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}:=\{x\in\mathcal{X}\mapsto\mathbbm{1}[x\in\bigcup_{j\geq i}A_{j}]:i\geq 1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The main point is that for any <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, this function class essentially encapsulates the previous construction for<math id="A6.SS1.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{n}=2^{-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{n}=2^{-n}</annotation></semantics></math>, by focusing only on the subclass <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{n}:=\{x\in\mathcal{X}\mapsto\mathbbm{1}[x\in\bigcup_{j\geq i}A_{j}]:i\in[2^{n},2^{n}+K_{n}]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{n}:=\{x\in\mathcal{X}\mapsto\mathbbm{1}[x\in\bigcup_{j\geq i}A_{j}]:i\in[2^{n},2^{n}+K_{n}]\}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}:=\left\lfloor 2^{n}/m\right\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}:=\left\lfloor 2^{n}/m\right\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>. The only slight modification in the construction of the PAC learner <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for the thresholds on <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[K_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[K_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> is that we additionally always label <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}^{\prime}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}^{\prime}=1</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\in\bigcup_{j&lt;2^{n}}A_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\in\bigcup_{j&lt;2^{n}}A_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, to preserve realizability in that case. Following the exact same arguments therefore shows that if there is an <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-differentially private <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>-accurate algorithm for <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> on distributions in <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> with sample complexity <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, then for any <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, there is an <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m25" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(2\alpha/\eta,\beta+mK_{n}\gamma_{n}\leq 2\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>α</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2\alpha/\eta,\beta+mK_{n}\gamma_{n}\leq 2\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>-accurate PAC learner for <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. This ends the proof.
 <math id="A6.SS1.p8.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\blacksquare" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\blacksquare</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Combining this result with the sample complexity lower bound from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, Theorem 1]</cite> for privately learning thresholds yields the following result.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 19</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem19.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem19.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is a constant <math id="Thmtheorem19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that the following holds. Let <math id="Thmtheorem19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> be a convex distribution class, <math id="Thmtheorem19.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem19.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. If for any generalized threshold class <math id="Thmtheorem19.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a <math id="Thmtheorem19.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0.1,\frac{c_{0}}{m^{2}\log m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0.1,\frac{c_{0}}{m^{2}\log m})</annotation></semantics></math>-differentially private <math id="Thmtheorem19.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon/32,1/32)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon/32,1/32)</annotation></semantics></math>-accurate algorithm on distributions from <math id="Thmtheorem19.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, with sample complexity <math id="Thmtheorem19.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>; then</span></p>
<table id="A6.Ex72" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex72.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq\Omega(\log^{\star}N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq\Omega(\log^{\star}N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem19.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem19.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem19.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Also, if <math id="Thmtheorem19.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is a convex distribution such that <math id="Thmtheorem19.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)=\infty</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="Thmtheorem19.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, then there exists a generalized threshold class <math id="Thmtheorem19.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any <math id="Thmtheorem19.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, there does not exist a <math id="Thmtheorem19.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0.1,\frac{c_{0}}{m^{2}\log m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0.1,\frac{c_{0}}{m^{2}\log m})</annotation></semantics></math>-differentially private <math id="Thmtheorem19.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon/32,1/32)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon/32,1/32)</annotation></semantics></math>-accurate algorithm on distributions from <math id="Thmtheorem19.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, with sample complexity <math id="Thmtheorem19.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.SS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof  </span>Given such an algorithm for generalized threshold classes, <a href="#Thmtheorem18" title="Lemma 18. ‣ F.1 Proof of ‣ Appendix F Proofs from ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> constructs a <math id="A6.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0.1,\frac{c_{0}}{m^{2}\log m}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0.1,\frac{c_{0}}{m^{2}\log m}))</annotation></semantics></math>-differentially private <math id="A6.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/16,1/16)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/16,1/16)</annotation></semantics></math>-accurate PAC learner for thresholds on <math id="A6.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[K]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[K]</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A6.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\Omega(\sqrt{N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)/m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\Omega(\sqrt{N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)/m})</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, for <math id="A6.SS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently small, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, Theorem 1]</cite> shows that</p>
<table id="A6.Ex73" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex73.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq\Omega(\log^{\star}K)=\Omega(\log^{\star}N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)-\log^{\star}m)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq\Omega(\log^{\star}K)=\Omega(\log^{\star}N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)-\log^{\star}m).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS1.p10.2" class="ltx_p">In turn, this shows the desired bound <math id="A6.SS1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq\Omega(\log^{\star}N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq\Omega(\log^{\star}N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The second claim is proved similarly using the second claim from <a href="#Thmtheorem18" title="Lemma 18. ‣ F.1 Proof of ‣ Appendix F Proofs from ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>.
 <math id="A6.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\blacksquare" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\blacksquare</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">We are now ready to prove <a href="#Thmtheorem11" title="Theorem 11. ‣ 6 Differential Privacy ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> as an immediate consequence of <a href="#Thmtheorem19" title="Theorem 19. ‣ F.1 Proof of ‣ Appendix F Proofs from ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a> and the previous characterization of generalized-smooth distribution classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.SS1.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof  of <a href="#Thmtheorem11" title="Theorem 11. ‣ 6 Differential Privacy ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a></span>
If <math id="A6.SS1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is not generalized smooth, then <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2. ‣ 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> implies that <math id="A6.SS1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a continuous submeasure, which in turn implies that there is <math id="A6.SS1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="A6.SS1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathcal{U}}(\epsilon)=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the desired impossibility result for private learnability is a direct consequence from <a href="#Thmtheorem19" title="Theorem 19. ‣ F.1 Proof of ‣ Appendix F Proofs from ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>.
 <math id="A6.SS1.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\blacksquare" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\blacksquare</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.2 </span>Proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem12" title="Theorem 12 (Private Learnability under Generalized Smoothness). ‣ 6 Differential Privacy ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a></h3>

<div id="A6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proof follows the same structure as private learning algorithms for VC classes under smoothness and with unlabelled public data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> but we include it here for completeness.
First, recall that under generalized smoothness, that any VC class has finite uniform cover from <a href="#Thmdefinition7" title="Definition 7 (Uniform Cover). ‣ 3.1 Uniform Covers ‣ 3 Learnability and Generalized Smoothness ‣ Characterizing Online and Private Learnability under Distributional Constraints via Generalized Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.
We present this result below for completeness.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem20" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 20</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem20.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Uniform Covers under Generalized Smoothness)<span id="Thmtheorem20.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem20.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem20.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem20.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="Thmtheorem20.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\rho,\mu_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\rho,\mu_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> generalized smooth class of distributions on <math id="Thmtheorem20.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, for any function class <math id="Thmtheorem20.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> of VC dimension <math id="Thmtheorem20.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, any <math id="Thmtheorem20.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a class <math id="Thmtheorem20.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> which is an <math id="Thmtheorem20.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-uniform cover of <math id="Thmtheorem20.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="Thmtheorem20.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and satisfies</span></p>
<table id="A6.Ex74" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log|\mathcal{F}_{\epsilon}|\leq d\log(C\cdot\rho^{-1}(\epsilon))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log|\mathcal{F}_{\epsilon}|\leq d\log(C\cdot\rho^{-1}(\epsilon))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem20.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem20.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for some universal constant <math id="Thmtheorem20.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof  </span>The idea is to construct an <math id="A6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-cover of <math id="A6.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the base measure <math id="A6.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any <math id="A6.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f,f^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f,f^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> we have that <math id="A6.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\left(\left\{x:f(x)\neq f^{\prime}(x)\right\}\right)\leq\rho(\mu_{0}\left(\left\{x:f(x)\neq f^{\prime}(x)\right\}\right))\leq\rho(\epsilon^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\left(\left\{x:f(x)\neq f^{\prime}(x)\right\}\right)\leq\rho(\mu_{0}\left(\left\{x:f(x)\neq f^{\prime}(x)\right\}\right))\leq\rho(\epsilon^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. Setting <math id="A6.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}=\rho^{-1}(\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}=\rho^{-1}(\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>, we see that any <math id="A6.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-cover with respect to <math id="A6.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="A6.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-uniform cover with respect to <math id="A6.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. The result then follows from the classical VC dimension covering number bound.
 <math id="A6.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\blacksquare" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\blacksquare</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The required private learning algorithm is then a direct application of the private learning algorithm based on the exponential mechanism from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> using uniform covers. We recall the main result below for completeness.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem21" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 21</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>)<span id="Thmtheorem21.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem21.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem21.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite function class. For any <math id="Thmtheorem21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon,\delta\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon,\delta\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an <math id="Thmtheorem21.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>-differentially private algorithm that is <math id="Thmtheorem21.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>-accurate for learning <math id="Thmtheorem21.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> with sample complexity</span></p>
<table id="A6.Ex75" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex75.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\frac{d+\log(1/\delta)}{\epsilon^{2}}+\frac{\log|\mathcal{F}|}{\alpha\epsilon}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\frac{d+\log(1/\delta)}{\epsilon^{2}}+\frac{\log|\mathcal{F}|}{\alpha\epsilon}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">and note that a <math id="A6.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\alpha,0\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\alpha,0\right)</annotation></semantics></math> private algorithm <math id="A6.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)</annotation></semantics></math> accurate learning algorithm for <math id="A6.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is also a <math id="A6.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\alpha,0\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\alpha,0\right)</annotation></semantics></math> private algorithm <math id="A6.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(2\epsilon,\delta\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(2\epsilon,\delta\right)</annotation></semantics></math> accurate learning algorithm for <math id="A6.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> for any distribution with marginal in <math id="A6.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</article>
